WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.440
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to CME 295, transformers

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.520
and large language models.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.960
My name is Afshine, and
I'll be teaching this class

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.600
with Shervine,
who's in the back.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.720
So before we start,
I just want to say

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.240
a few words about ourselves.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.280
So Shervine and I
are twin brothers,

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.400
and we have very
similar backgrounds.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.960
So we both went to a school in
France called Centrale Paris.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.800
And then we came to
the US for grad school.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.960
So on my end, I went to MIT.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.720
And then Shervine came here at
Stanford in the ICME department.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.560
And then after that, we both
went to the tech industry.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.560
So we first both went to Uber,
and then we went to Google.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.640
And now we're both at Netflix.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.320
So Shervine and I, we like LLMs.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.239
So we've been working
in the field of LLMs,

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.500
I would say since
the early 2020s.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.020
And so that's one
of the reasons why

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.260
we're so lucky to be
teaching this class.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:17.220
We've been teaching it since
2021 under various formats.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.180
But as attention
gained towards LLMs,

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.100
we have actually made
it an actual Stanford

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.860
class since last year.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.260
And this session is
the third instance.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.420
And so if you're here wondering
if this class is good for you--

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.860
so the goal of this class
is to tell you how LLMs work

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:47.020
and in particular, understand
the underlying mechanism of its

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.060
architecture-- so we're going
to see the transformer--

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.700
but also how LLMs are
trained, how we can use them,

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.230
what they're good at,
what they're not good at.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.750
So hopefully, you'll be
able to get a sense of all

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.150
of that in this class.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.930
And in terms of the
target audience,

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.590
I would say it's a
pretty wide audience.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:12.970
So in case you want to go
and be a research scientist,

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.270
I would say it could be a
great class to just open up

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.830
the set of things that you
are able to see in terms of,

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.430
what are the different
techniques that are being used,

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.190
what are the different
applications, and so on.

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:28.870
So I think that's one.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:33.050
The second one is if you want
to do a personal project.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:36.250
So nowadays, coding
agents are everywhere.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:40.290
And so if you want to just take
one tool and just do something,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.030
you should probably know
what the coding agent

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.230
is good at and not good at.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:48.990
And third, I would say
no matter your role,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:53.050
there is a growing need
of being literate in AI.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.770
So there's this term
called AI literacy.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.690
So no matter where you
work and your role,

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:05.090
I would say just knowing how
these tools work is becoming

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.530
increasingly important.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.250
So if you feel like you're
in one of these categories,

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.970
this class may be good for you.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.570
And in terms of
the prerequisites,

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:20.310
I would say if you have some
foundations in linear algebra,

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.930
if you know what a matrix is,
how we do multiplications,

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.570
then that would be great--
along with some basics in ML.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.590
So for instance, what
is a loss function,

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:33.610
what is a neural network, what
is an embedding, although we

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.230
will be seeing
that in the class.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:41.330
But it's good to have those,
I would say, beforehand.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.690
Make sense?

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.170
OK, cool.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:50.390
So you may or may not know that
this class is being recorded.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.570
So you can see the
camera in the back.

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.750
It was actually
recorded last year.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.878
So you may be
wondering, well, what

00:03:56.878 --> 00:03:58.670
is the point in attending
the lecture if it

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.190
was already recorded?

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:04.070
So the field is
moving so quickly

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.750
that there has been so
many new things that

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.150
have come up since last year.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.030
And actually, one of
the goals of this class

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:17.029
is to incorporate all the
advances that have happened.

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:18.510
So I'll name a few.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:23.730
So there has been some progress
on the post-training side.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.130
So for instance, on
policy distillation,

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:28.650
if you've heard
of it, of course,

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:32.190
AI agents, so coding
agents and so on,

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:35.450
and new paradigms of LLMs.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:39.490
So traditionally, it was
predicting tokens one at a time.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:43.830
But nowadays, there's been an
increasing number of models

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.010
that have approached
things differently,

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:51.320
so from the diffusion
perspective, among other things.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.120
So long story short, if
you're attending this class

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.160
and you're preparing
for exams, please

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.240
look at this year's
lectures because they

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.000
will be quite different.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.760
In terms of the logistics--

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.700
so I'm so happy to see so many
of you on a Friday afternoon.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:14.480
So it will be every
Friday from 3:30 to 5:20.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.680
It will be in this class.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.680
And then it is a two-unit class.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.360
You can either choose it to
take it as letter or credit,

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.480
noncredit.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.180
So again, all the
lectures are recorded.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.680
We'll make sure to
post the recordings,

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:36.600
at most, two days after
the lecture has happened.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.200
So feel free to watch
it remotely if you want

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.912
or attend the class in person.

00:05:42.912 --> 00:05:44.620
Actually, we'd love
to see you in person,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.600
so feel free to
choose either way.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:56.500
And this class has no
homeworks, but it has two exams.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:00.340
So one is the midterm,
and one is the final.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:05.260
So the midterm will be about
the first four lectures,

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.920
and it will be one
month from now, I guess?

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.860
Yeah, October 23.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.920
And the final exam will be
on the last five lectures.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.140
