00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:10.900
Cool.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:13.040
Another thing-- I mean,
something that I personally

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.760
have felt when I first
started in the NLP field

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.960
a few years ago,
there are so many

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.680
abbreviations all
over the place.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:24.960
And it can be intimidating.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:27.800
So our hope is
that in case you're

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:31.040
feeling overwhelmed today
by these abbreviations,

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.440
that by the end of
this class, you'll

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:39.020
have a mental mapping of which
abbreviations correspond to what

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:40.020
concepts.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:42.380
So there is our goal.

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:48.820
And with that, we're going
to start by asking ourselves

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.660
how we got here.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:58.500
So 10-15 years ago, in
the 2010's, so I remember,

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:01.220
the field was
completely different.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:03.700
We didn't have one model
that did everything.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:07.100
We had typically one
model per use case.

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:10.460
So the kind of model that
was dominating the field

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:13.840
was RNNs, Recurrent
Neural Networks,

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.220
which we'll briefly
see in this lecture.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:21.140
And you had these use
case specific models,

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:23.840
such as for
sentiment extraction.

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:27.700
So you ask yourself if
a sentence, a review

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:30.380
is positive,
negative, or neutral.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:32.860
If you had a text you
wanted to translate,

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:35.440
you had a dedicated
model for that.

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:36.540
And if you had--

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:40.500
I don't know-- text, you wanted
to identify names, locations,

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:44.520
and so on, you would also have
a dedicated model for that.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:46.800
So this was in 2010's.

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:50.120
Performance was OK.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:54.200
But the thing that really
made a true difference that

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:58.760
was a turning point was 2017.

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:02.680
So 2017, there's a
paper that came out,

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:06.040
that we're going to talk
about at length today,

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:09.680
that's called "Attention
is All You Need,"

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:13.920
that introduced a new
architecture that relied

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:18.920
on a paradigm, that proved
to be very scalable.

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:22.800
By scalable, I mean,
if you give your model

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:27.440
more data, more computes,
and more parameters,

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:30.800
you're actually able to
see performance gains that

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:35.108
are far greater compared to
what you used to see before.

00:14:35.108 --> 00:14:37.150
And it's something that
we're going to see today.

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:41.570
So this architecture is
called the transformer.

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:44.090
And on the right, you
have an illustration

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:47.870
of how it's illustrated
in the original paper.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:50.530
We're going to see how it works.

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:55.490
And from there, people have
taken this architecture

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:59.910
and just scaled it from
a model size perspective

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:04.690
but also from the number of
tokens it's been trained on.

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:10.530
And this is how modern-day LLMs
have come into the picture,

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:12.450
lLMs that actually know--

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:15.430
I mean, that actually
generate texts pretty well,

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:18.050
that generate code pretty well.

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:23.430
And in particular, in
2022, ChatGPT got released.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:25.070
So I remember in
November, I believe.

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:27.890
Yeah.

00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:30.030
And again, it was
a turning point

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.890
from more of a
product perspective

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:34.430
because before that
date, people were not

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:38.110
really used to interacting
with the chatbot.

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:41.630
So I believe that release
was really the first time

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:44.870
that people got
introduced to that aspect.

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:47.950
And I think even the
person or the company

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:49.910
who launched it
probably didn't even

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:51.730
realize how big it would get.

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:56.230
I mean, I'm assuming because
in their tweets that released

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:59.630
the model, I believe
they had a typo, which

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:01.630
I find funny because
nowadays, whenever

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:06.710
there is a model release,
there is 10 times review

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:08.410
of what the wording is.

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:11.190
So I'm assuming
that this was not

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:13.090
the case for the
announcement here.

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:14.250
But yeah.

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:17.670
So that is also
another turning point.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:22.110
And then here we are, 2026.

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:26.030
I'm assuming a lot
of you or all of you

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:30.210
are interacting with coding
agents almost on a daily basis.

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:33.410
So we're going to see how
we got to that stage, how

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:39.170
we went from a conversational
LLM, conversational assistant,

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:43.370
to actually agentic behavior.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:44.970
So we're going to
see that, especially

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:47.310
in the second part
of this class.

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:54.330
So far so good?

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:55.830
Cool.

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:59.650
So with that, we're
going to start

00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:06.730
with one foundational concept,
which is tokenization.

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:09.210
So what is tokenization?

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:14.210
So when we talk about text,
text is not really something

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:16.250
that models can understand.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:19.450
Models understand numbers.

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:22.490
They don't understand text.

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:26.819
So the first goal
is to find a way

00:17:26.819 --> 00:17:30.220
to represent that text
in forms of something

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:34.220
that models can understand,
so in forms of numbers.

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:37.140
So the way that we
do that is we first

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:42.740
try to divide the
text into entities

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:46.140
that we can then represent.

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:48.420
And so the process
of dividing the text

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:53.620
into indivisible entities is
what is called tokenization.

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:58.300
So in this example, we have "A
cute Teddy bear is reading."

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:05.500
And one possible tokenization
is to say that "A" is one

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:06.980
indivisible unit.

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:09.260
"Cute" is another unit.

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:11.540
"Teddy bear" can
be another unit.

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:15.100
And so on and so forth.

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:21.260
So each of these elements
is called a token.

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:26.120
And our goal is to
represent these tokens

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:28.800
with mathematical
representations that

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:30.760
are meaningful.

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:32.600
So in this case,
we want each token

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:36.700
to be represented by a
vector, a vector of numbers.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:46.560
So in order for you to
tokenize an input text,

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:49.600
you need to know, what
are the tokens that are

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:52.040
allowed for you to be using?

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:53.720
And so we have
something that's called

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:58.080
a vocabulary which includes
all possible tokens

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:00.680
that you can use.

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:04.540
And depending on the
vocabulary that you choose,

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:09.480
you may end up with
different sequence length.

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:11.540
So here, by sequence
length, I mean

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:15.300
the number of tokens, by
which you divided your text.

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:16.820
So for instance,
in this instance,

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:21.700
we have 1, 2, 3, 4,
5, 6, six tokens.

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:24.060
We're going to see in
a second that there

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:28.980
is a tight relationship between
vocabulary size and sequence

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:30.620
length, and that's
something that

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:34.800
will inform which tokenizer
we should be choosing.

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:39.740
So far, so good?

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:43.040
So now, you may be wondering,
well, all of that is great,

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:47.500
but how do I know how
I tokenize my text?

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:52.080
This is just a theoretical
concept, but how do I do that?

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:56.000
So one natural way is
for you to say, OK,

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:01.240
so I'll just tokenize the
texts with respect to words.

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:03.860
So I'm going to
consider space as being

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:07.460
a delimiter between tokens.

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:09.720
So here, I would say "A
cute Teddy bear is reading."

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:16.140
So every word in that text would
actually represent a token.

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:19.040
So what are the pros and
cons of this approach?

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:23.640
So pros, very simple, especially
for the English language,

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:27.680
you map a token to its
words, very simple.

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:30.780
It is also
interpretable, as in you

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:33.480
know when you get a
representation of a word that it

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:36.200
corresponds-- or a
representation of a token

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:40.520
that it corresponds
to a given word.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:45.800
However, at least
in English, you

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:48.120
have many variations of words.

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:53.280
So, for instance, a
noun, you can have it

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:55.180
as singular and plural.

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:58.520
You can have the masculine
form, the feminine form.

00:20:58.520 --> 00:21:00.720
You can have many,
many variations.

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:05.180
So your vocabulary size may get
very large as a result of that.

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:07.400
So that's one con.

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:11.320
The second con is, we're
going to see in a second,

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:16.400
that when we learn the
representation of each token,

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:18.210
there is nothing
that guarantees us

00:21:18.210 --> 00:21:22.890
that two tokens that
represent the same thing

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:24.830
would get the same
representation.

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:29.330
So, for instance, if you
consider the word "bear"

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:33.730
and the word "bears," these
are the singular and plural

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:36.690
of the same animal,
which is a bear.

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:40.250
And so you wouldn't want the
representation of these two

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:43.030
tokens to be too
far from one another

00:21:43.030 --> 00:21:46.210
because they represent
the same thing.

00:21:46.210 --> 00:21:50.130
Well, with this way of
tokenizing your text,

00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:54.290
you would have a hard
time, first of all,

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:55.970
leveraging the
knowledge that you

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:59.850
have from the similar
token, and second, just

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:04.370
guaranteeing that these
representations are similar.

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:09.690
And then the third one is, well,
given that you need to learn

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:13.470
the representation of
each of these words--

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:15.830
so one word is one token--

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:21.950
you may run into a case where
if you train your tokenizer

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:26.470
on a given corpus of
text, that you actually

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:29.550
do not have a given
word that you then

00:22:29.550 --> 00:22:33.230
encounter at inference time.

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:36.590
So you may actually be
missing representations

00:22:36.590 --> 00:22:39.370
that you could not have
had the chance to learn,

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:42.910
just because you did not
encounter that word at training

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:44.190
time.

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:50.370
And therefore, you can run into
the risk of out-of-vocabulary,

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:56.070
meaning that a token is actually
not something that you have seen

00:22:56.070 --> 00:22:58.470
at training time, and you
don't have the representation

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:00.750
for that one.

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:05.050
So long story short, word-level
tokenization is simple,

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:08.750
but it comes with a few cons.

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:11.910
And one of the cons
is really the fact

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:18.330
that you are not able to
leverage words of similar roots.

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:21.850
And this leads to vocabulary
being large and risk

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:23.090
out-of-vocabulary.

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:30.670
So the next question is,
OK, can we do better?

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:33.930
Is there another way of
tokenizing the text that

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:35.810
could make more sense?

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:42.510
So a natural thought could be,
well, you don't divide by words,

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:45.090
you divide by a character.

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:48.290
You have a fixed number of
allowed characters, at least

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:51.170
in English or in any languages.

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:57.090
And so you are not running
the risk out-of-vocabulary

00:23:57.090 --> 00:24:00.930
anymore because you will
encounter all the letters

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:05.410
in the alphabet in your training
set, or you can try to do that.

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:09.930
And by doing so, you will
also have a small vocabulary.

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:15.940
And you would also be robust
to casing and misspellings

00:24:15.940 --> 00:24:19.060
that people usually make when--

00:24:19.060 --> 00:24:21.760
I mean, I'm not sure if you
recognize yourself, I do.

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:22.860
So when I type--

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:27.080
I don't know-- on my computer,
I sometimes misspell things.

00:24:27.080 --> 00:24:29.500
And I wouldn't want
the representation

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:35.220
of a word I meant to write to be
different from the actual word.

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.900
And so here, we
would not be going

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:42.460
into the case of not
recognizing a word just

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:45.780
because it is misspelled.

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:53.980
But we will see that if you do
that, the sequence length is

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:58.380
going to get much longer.

00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:04.260
And model complexity is a
function of sequence length.

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:06.660
So the sequence is very long.

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:10.360
Then your model will take a long
time to process the sequence.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:14.840
So it will make
computations much slower.

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:19.900
And the embeddings themselves,
they're much less interpretable

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:22.360
as in-- if we tell
you the letter

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:25.560
A has this
representation, it's maybe

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:31.080
much more abstract than talking
about word representation.

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:32.620
And we will see
that in a second.

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:39.040
So far, does that make sense?

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:40.920
Yeah.

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:46.840
So I hope I am motivating
a trade-off enough

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:49.320
that the next natural
thought in your mind

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:51.340
is to actually try
something in between,

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:55.800
so not word, not character,
but in between, so subwords.

00:25:55.800 --> 00:26:00.480
So how about we
tokenize the text

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:05.080
by leveraging
knowledge that we have

00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:11.100
about words that we can learn
from some training corpus?

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:16.740
And we divide common
occurrences of letters,

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:18.360
and we represent
them with a token.

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:22.620
So for instance, for reading,
we would represent "read"

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:26.460
with a token and "ing"
with another token.

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:28.715
So how about we do that?

00:26:28.715 --> 00:26:30.340
So the good thing
here is that we would

00:26:30.340 --> 00:26:32.180
leverage the root of words.

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:36.180
So in my previous example,
"bear" and "bears"

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:41.460
would have the, for instance,
"bear" token in common.

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:43.900
And then the second thing is
it would be actually learned

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:50.220
from the data, as in the way you
tokenize text is informed by how

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:53.420
words are actually written.

00:26:53.420 --> 00:26:56.360
But then the con is, well, you
need to do that extra work.

00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:58.380
So in the word-level
case, you just

00:26:58.380 --> 00:27:03.600
need to produce tokens by
just dividing based on space.

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:06.670
So here, you would do-- you
need to do some extra work.

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:11.310
And then the way you
obtain these tokens will

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:16.870
be highly dependent on the
type of data that you have

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:20.450
when you do your
tokenizer training.

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:22.590
So that's another
thing, another con.

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:24.750
So we need to make sure
that the data that you

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:28.350
use to learn how
to tokenize things

00:27:28.350 --> 00:27:31.210
is actually aligned
with your end goal.

00:27:35.630 --> 00:27:40.430
So I just want to
say something here.

00:27:40.430 --> 00:27:43.930
So I talked about three
ways of tokenizing texts.

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:50.230
So word level, character level,
subword level Among the three,

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:54.270
subword level is by far
the most popular nowadays,

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:56.910
for all the reasons
that I mentioned.

00:27:56.910 --> 00:28:02.430
And you'll hear the word
or the acronym BPE a lot.

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:06.210
So BPE is a type of
subword tokenizer

00:28:06.210 --> 00:28:09.770
that stands for
Byte Pair Encoding.

00:28:09.770 --> 00:28:12.527
So the paper is linked at the
bottom of the slide in case

00:28:12.527 --> 00:28:14.610
you're interested to learn
more, but I'll give you

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:17.570
just the gist of how it works.

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:24.370
So BPE learns what tokens
should be in your vocabulary

00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:29.690
by starting from an
initial set of tokens

00:28:29.690 --> 00:28:37.050
and identifying the most
frequent pairs in your training

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:42.010
data and by coming up
with dedicated tokens

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:45.510
for merged tokens
of these pairs.

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:47.270
So for instance--
I'll give an example.

00:28:47.270 --> 00:28:51.490
Let's imagine your initial
vocabulary is all the letters

00:28:51.490 --> 00:28:53.090
in the alphabet.

00:28:53.090 --> 00:29:01.590
And you have, I don't know,
the word "an," like "an idea,"

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:02.850
"an amazing course."

00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:05.450
So "an," that occurs
very frequently.

00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:10.230
So here, it will say, OK, "a"
and "an" appear very frequently

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:10.810
together.

00:29:10.810 --> 00:29:15.870
So let's actually add a
token that merges the two.

00:29:15.870 --> 00:29:18.870
It will add that new
token in the vocabulary,

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:24.430
and then repeat the process
until reaching a desired

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:26.990
final vocabulary size.

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:31.070
And that will be your
final vocabulary.

00:29:31.070 --> 00:29:33.790
So the nice thing is that
these tokens are learned

00:29:33.790 --> 00:29:36.630
from the data, so things
that appear together

00:29:36.630 --> 00:29:40.290
are actually identified and
added to your vocabulary.

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:44.810
So this will lead to your
sequence length being shorter.

00:29:44.810 --> 00:29:47.070
So you will not have
to decompose your text

00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:48.910
into that many tokens.

00:29:48.910 --> 00:29:55.110
So your model will not take that
much time to process your texts.

00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:57.650
And so that's where the
efficiency comes from.

00:29:57.650 --> 00:30:00.970
So if you have a
training corpus that has,

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:04.610
let's say, a lot of the words
that you encounter in practice,

00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:07.610
then the sequence
length of the tokens

00:30:07.610 --> 00:30:11.290
you will get from a
piece of text that

00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:15.130
uses the same words as what you
had in your training corpus,

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:19.610
will be much shorter.

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:23.910
So in case you're doing
multilingual work,

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:26.170
then make sure that your
tokenizer is trained

00:30:26.170 --> 00:30:30.130
on a corpus that represents
all the languages that you're

00:30:30.130 --> 00:30:31.610
interested in.

00:30:31.610 --> 00:30:32.630
That's one example.

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:37.550
So that was a lot.

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:39.150
So I'll just pause for a second.

00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:46.130
Is there any questions
so far on this part?

00:30:46.130 --> 00:30:47.730
Yes.

00:30:47.730 --> 00:30:51.610
So the question is,
what is the relationship

00:30:51.610 --> 00:30:53.230
between BPE and n-gram?

00:30:53.230 --> 00:30:55.130
So n-gram is actually
something separate.

00:30:55.130 --> 00:30:57.090
And this one, yes--
to your point,

00:30:57.090 --> 00:31:01.700
it will learn the
tokens from your corpus

00:31:01.700 --> 00:31:04.940
by merging the most frequent
pairs in an iterative way

00:31:04.940 --> 00:31:10.820
until reaching a final
desired vocabulary size.

00:31:10.820 --> 00:31:14.580
So next natural question is, OK,
you have your tokens, then now,

00:31:14.580 --> 00:31:16.580
how do you know how
to represent them?

00:31:16.580 --> 00:31:18.300
Maybe you're asking
yourself this.

00:31:18.300 --> 00:31:22.140
And we will see that actually
in about five minutes.

00:31:22.140 --> 00:31:24.763
So more on that in a bit.

00:31:24.763 --> 00:31:26.180
Actually, I'm
looking at the time.

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:29.840
I don't think I'm on time,
so I'll probably move on.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:31.460
But if you have any
questions, please

00:31:31.460 --> 00:31:34.420
feel free to interrupt me.

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:37.220
Tokenization is really the
building block of everything

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:40.760
we'll see from here, so yeah.

00:31:44.340 --> 00:31:47.860
So far, we've seen tokens
that represent texts

00:31:47.860 --> 00:31:49.320
that we want to tokenize.

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:52.780
But the truth is we also
have some special tokens.

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:54.940
And these special
tokens are meant

00:31:54.940 --> 00:32:00.000
to represent things that we
want to express in sequence.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:03.600
So one such token is
whenever we encounter

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:07.200
a term or a piece that is
actually not something that

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:09.320
is part of our vocabulary.

00:32:09.320 --> 00:32:13.840
So the convention is to have a
dedicated token that represents

00:32:13.840 --> 00:32:18.120
this out-of-vocabulary
piece of text,

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:20.960
and it's called
the unknown token.

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:23.120
So it's one special token.

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:25.280
So I give an example,
"Teddy bear."

00:32:25.280 --> 00:32:29.080
Imagine if I write
it Teddy DI bear.

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:30.960
And in the training
corpus, we've

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:32.580
never seen such a spelling.

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:40.500
Then if your vocabulary is only
composed of these four tokens,

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:43.080
so Teddy, cute,
bear, and unknown,

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:48.040
then Teddy bear will be
tokenized as unknown token,

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:50.280
and then bear.

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:52.400
One example--

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:58.180
Another set of special
tokens-- so a lot of the times,

00:32:58.180 --> 00:33:01.780
you need to indicate
when the sequence is

00:33:01.780 --> 00:33:05.860
starting, when it is ending,
and we'll see how we do that.

00:33:05.860 --> 00:33:08.240
So we have two sets
of special tokens.

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:12.620
One is beginning of
sequence, BOS, and then

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:15.060
end of sequence, EOS.

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:19.220
Those are mostly used when
we want to generate text.

00:33:19.220 --> 00:33:23.380
So for instance, when you
want to start the generation,

00:33:23.380 --> 00:33:26.420
the convention is
to feed your model

00:33:26.420 --> 00:33:28.580
with the beginning
of sequence token,

00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:30.820
just to indicate to your
token-- to your model

00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:33.060
that you want to generate text.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:37.320
Your model will then, at
the end of its generation,

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:39.860
just output the end
of sequence token

00:33:39.860 --> 00:33:44.660
to just tell you that its
generation is finished.

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:46.580
So this is how it's used.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:49.840
So "What a cute Teddy bear"
would be beginning of sequence,

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:53.110
"What a cute Teddy bear" and
end of sequence, as an example.

00:33:53.110 --> 00:33:55.390
And then the last one
that you may encounter

00:33:55.390 --> 00:33:57.010
is the padding token.

00:33:57.010 --> 00:34:02.270
And that one is meant
to make your sequences

00:34:02.270 --> 00:34:05.710
be of a certain size.

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:11.230
So this is sometimes used
for efficiency perspectives.

00:34:11.230 --> 00:34:14.469
If you want all your sequences
to be of the same length,

00:34:14.469 --> 00:34:16.969
because we'll see that we can
represent that with vectors,

00:34:16.969 --> 00:34:18.949
matrices, tensors--

00:34:18.949 --> 00:34:24.310
and hardware loves things
that have the same dimension--

00:34:24.310 --> 00:34:28.469
then padding can also be used.

00:34:28.469 --> 00:34:34.429
But I want to say that this
list is by no means exhaustive

00:34:34.429 --> 00:34:39.310
and that you also have a
lot of different conventions

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:40.670
that people take.

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:43.929
So this way of
writing, BOS, EOS,

00:34:43.929 --> 00:34:48.630
this way of writing
padding, it's not universal.

00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:50.530
So just don't be
surprised if you

00:34:50.530 --> 00:34:53.570
encounter different variations.

00:34:53.570 --> 00:34:57.550
And nowadays, you have
things like assistants.

00:34:57.550 --> 00:34:59.750
So you have a user, assistant.

00:34:59.750 --> 00:35:02.130
And those are other
things that people

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:04.570
represent with special tokens.

00:35:04.570 --> 00:35:07.330
So again, this is not
an exhaustive list.

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:10.730
But just know that
such tokens exist,

00:35:10.730 --> 00:35:14.290
and they are meant to convey
something about the text

00:35:14.290 --> 00:35:16.630
that the model
should understand.

00:35:22.210 --> 00:35:24.090
Cool.

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:28.010
That's it for tokenization.

00:35:28.010 --> 00:35:30.050
Any questions?

00:35:30.050 --> 00:35:31.170
Yes.

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:32.730
The question is,
what is the order

00:35:32.730 --> 00:35:35.790
of magnitude of the vocabulary
size for LLMs right now?

00:35:35.790 --> 00:35:37.170
So it's a great question.

00:35:37.170 --> 00:35:41.770
It is on the order of
hundreds of thousands.

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:44.990
So you will see something like
hundreds of thousands, 200,000,

00:35:44.990 --> 00:35:46.330
something like this.

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:48.870
And these are
tokenizers that are

00:35:48.870 --> 00:35:54.630
able to handle both English
but also other languages.

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:58.710
And as I mentioned, the most
common type of tokenizer

00:35:58.710 --> 00:36:00.470
nowadays is the BPE one.

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:05.190
So yeah, so these are the things
that are good to have in mind.

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:06.790
Yeah.

00:36:06.790 --> 00:36:10.910
So the question is, having
multiple languages be handled

00:36:10.910 --> 00:36:13.550
by your tokenizer, does that
make your tokenizer a bit less

00:36:13.550 --> 00:36:15.230
of an expert in
a given language?

00:36:15.230 --> 00:36:18.030
I think it is hard
to say, I would say,

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:22.830
because there may be some
synergies between languages.

00:36:22.830 --> 00:36:25.690
So I would say it's
hard to say in general.

00:36:25.690 --> 00:36:29.270
But that being
said, the vocabulary

00:36:29.270 --> 00:36:35.270
size that you fix when you
run your BPE algorithm will

00:36:35.270 --> 00:36:38.790
be filled with tokens
from different languages.

00:36:38.790 --> 00:36:48.320
So you may get a vocabulary that
may lead you to longer sequence

00:36:48.320 --> 00:36:52.760
length if you're only interested
in one particular language.

00:36:52.760 --> 00:36:55.060
So from an efficiency
perspective,

00:36:55.060 --> 00:36:57.120
maybe you'll see something.

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:59.920
But then from a
representation perspective,

00:36:59.920 --> 00:37:01.020
it's not super clear.

00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:05.760
So one example is
acronyms of organizations

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:08.280
that maybe something
that people talk

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:10.080
about in a bunch of languages.

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:14.040
So you may benefit from
learning that representation

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:19.880
from different texts of
different languages, so pros

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:20.500
and cons.

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:26.560
Great.

00:37:26.560 --> 00:37:27.060
Cool.

00:37:27.060 --> 00:37:29.640
So now, what we
know, what we have

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:33.560
learned is given an
input text, we're

00:37:33.560 --> 00:37:38.720
able to generate tokens or
represent that text with tokens.

00:37:38.720 --> 00:37:44.620
So the next natural question is
how to represent those tokens.

00:37:44.620 --> 00:37:47.900
So this is what we're
going to see now.

00:37:47.900 --> 00:37:51.100
So the most naive
way to do that is

00:37:51.100 --> 00:37:55.740
to say that each
token is different.

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:57.080
So it's extremely obvious.

00:37:57.080 --> 00:37:58.580
But you can
represent your tokens

00:37:58.580 --> 00:38:02.180
by what we call
one-hot encodings.

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:05.280
So here, let's say I
have the token soft.

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:11.020
I can say, OK, it's
the 1,0,0 vector.

00:38:11.020 --> 00:38:13.900
And if you do that
for all your tokens,

00:38:13.900 --> 00:38:16.820
well, the bad thing
is they're all

00:38:16.820 --> 00:38:20.340
going to be orthogonal
to each other

00:38:20.340 --> 00:38:23.380
if you take the dot product.

00:38:23.380 --> 00:38:24.640
So is it a good thing?

00:38:28.340 --> 00:38:29.380
No.

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:31.740
It's not a good thing
because it doesn't tell you

00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:34.860
anything about what
these tokens are

00:38:34.860 --> 00:38:36.940
and how they're
related to each other.

00:38:36.940 --> 00:38:39.580
So it's not what you want.

00:38:39.580 --> 00:38:43.240
What you want is to
represent these tokens

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:50.360
in a way that reflects how
similar they are to one another.

00:38:50.360 --> 00:38:52.160
That is what you want.

00:38:52.160 --> 00:38:54.320
So, for instance,
you want "Teddy bear"

00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:58.560
to be similar to "soft"
because Teddy bears are soft,

00:38:58.560 --> 00:39:01.728
but maybe book to be
maybe independent.

00:39:01.728 --> 00:39:03.020
This is what you want to learn.

00:39:03.020 --> 00:39:06.880
You want to learn
meaningful representations.

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:09.880
And by the way, why
do you want to learn

00:39:09.880 --> 00:39:11.060
meaningful representations?

00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:13.518
Well, it's because you want to
do something with your text.

00:39:13.518 --> 00:39:15.880
You need to have it as
input into your model

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:18.200
in order to do
something as output--

00:39:18.200 --> 00:39:21.720
so for instance,
generating the next words,

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:22.820
predicting some class.

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:27.680
So you need to understand
what is in your text, which

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:30.880
is the reason why
what you want is

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:35.560
meaningful token
representations.

00:39:35.560 --> 00:39:42.930
So this is what an
earlier method called

00:39:42.930 --> 00:39:46.930
Word2vec tried to achieve.

00:39:46.930 --> 00:39:54.810
So here, the idea is to
learn these representations

00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:57.530
through what we
call a proxy task.

00:39:57.530 --> 00:40:00.995
So a proxy task is a task
that you're trying to learn,

00:40:00.995 --> 00:40:02.370
but it's not the
task that you're

00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:05.370
trying to learn as an end goal.

00:40:05.370 --> 00:40:07.650
It is a task that you
try to learn in order

00:40:07.650 --> 00:40:10.130
to achieve something else.

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:14.930
And here, the proxy task is
something like predicting

00:40:14.930 --> 00:40:18.950
the word that is in between
some context words--

00:40:18.950 --> 00:40:23.490
so this is the continuous bag
of words version of Word2vec--

00:40:23.490 --> 00:40:28.710
or predicting words that are
surrounding a given word.

00:40:28.710 --> 00:40:31.170
That is the skip-gram version.

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:34.930
So we'll go through an example
so that it becomes clear.

00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:38.870
So, does everyone know
what a neural network is?

00:40:38.870 --> 00:40:40.030
Yeah.

00:40:40.030 --> 00:40:40.530
OK.

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:44.230
So I represented a very
basic neural network.

00:40:44.230 --> 00:40:48.350
So you can think of it as an
input matrix multiplication

00:40:48.350 --> 00:40:53.990
to get a hidden representation
of size D and then again,

00:40:53.990 --> 00:40:58.070
matrix multiplication to go back
to size V or vocabulary size.

00:40:58.070 --> 00:41:03.830
So let's assume that you have
the sentence "A cute Teddy bear

00:41:03.830 --> 00:41:05.550
is reading."

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:07.850
And let's assume, for
the sake of simplicity,

00:41:07.850 --> 00:41:16.550
that your goal is to predict
"cute" from the word "a."

00:41:16.550 --> 00:41:18.390
So let's imagine that
your proxy task is

00:41:18.390 --> 00:41:22.110
to predict the next word.

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:29.270
So here, "a," you're very
naive representation of "a" is

00:41:29.270 --> 00:41:31.110
the one-hot encoding.

00:41:31.110 --> 00:41:35.270
So let's assume that you have
a vocabulary size of six,

00:41:35.270 --> 00:41:37.690
and "a" is your first token.

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:43.810
So you would represent
"a" with 1,0,0,0,0,0.

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:45.630
You pass it through
your network.

00:41:45.630 --> 00:41:49.330
So think of it as matrix
multiplication with this vector.

00:41:49.330 --> 00:41:52.970
You would have a
hidden state of size 2,

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:56.970
to assume two
numbers, 0.2 and 0.9.

00:41:56.970 --> 00:41:59.230
And then you pass it
through your network,

00:41:59.230 --> 00:42:00.990
so again, another
matrix multiplication.

00:42:00.990 --> 00:42:04.930
And then you have
predictions that sum to 1.

00:42:04.930 --> 00:42:09.530
So you can use something like
a softmax to normalize them.

00:42:09.530 --> 00:42:20.290
And your goal is to have these
probabilities be as close

00:42:20.290 --> 00:42:23.850
to the label that you have,
which is predicting the word

00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:24.410
"cute."

00:42:24.410 --> 00:42:26.930
And so "cute," let's suppose
that it's the second word

00:42:26.930 --> 00:42:27.870
of your vocabulary.

00:42:27.870 --> 00:42:31.936
So it is represented
by 0,1,0,0,0.

00:42:31.936 --> 00:42:38.550
Your goal is, like on the slide,
for the 0.4 to be as close as 1

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:41.510
as possible.

00:42:41.510 --> 00:42:45.950
So the idea is to have
a loss function that

00:42:45.950 --> 00:42:51.950
penalizes the fact of having
a probability of a given word

00:42:51.950 --> 00:42:54.870
being too far from the word
that you're trying to predict--

00:42:54.870 --> 00:42:58.230
so in case you know the
cross-entropy loss--

00:42:58.230 --> 00:43:02.350
and then updates the
weights of your model

00:43:02.350 --> 00:43:06.950
in order to reflect
what you want.

00:43:06.950 --> 00:43:09.270
So just so that we're
clear, I will just

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:12.390
walk through all the
quantities that we mentioned.

00:43:12.390 --> 00:43:16.030
So here, the one-hot encoding
that represents the token

00:43:16.030 --> 00:43:19.790
is of the vocabulary size.

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:24.630
The hidden state or also
called latent representation

00:43:24.630 --> 00:43:29.650
is of size D. And D is actually
a parameter that you can choose,

00:43:29.650 --> 00:43:33.640
typically much smaller
than the vocabulary size.

00:43:33.640 --> 00:43:36.040
And then the predicted
word probabilities--

00:43:36.040 --> 00:43:38.060
well, you have a
probability per token,

00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:40.740
so it needs to match
the vocabulary size.

00:43:40.740 --> 00:43:44.320
So it's of size V. And
it also matches the size

00:43:44.320 --> 00:43:46.080
of the one-hot encoding.

00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:50.560
And this is where
you're able to try

00:43:50.560 --> 00:43:52.920
to penalize for
deviations from the word

00:43:52.920 --> 00:43:54.840
that you're trying to predict.

00:43:54.840 --> 00:44:01.400
So let's assume you do that for
"a" trying to predict "cute."

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:04.840
You repeat the same thing
for "cute" for "Teddy bear."

00:44:04.840 --> 00:44:06.420
So you go "cute."

00:44:06.420 --> 00:44:08.525
You represent it with
the one-hot encoding.

00:44:08.525 --> 00:44:09.900
You pass it through
your network.

00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:13.680
You have a latent representation
and again, probabilities.

00:44:13.680 --> 00:44:17.080
You compare that with the actual
label that is representing

00:44:17.080 --> 00:44:18.240
"Teddy bear."

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:19.760
Compute the loss.

00:44:19.760 --> 00:44:26.840
Update the weights given how
far you are from the label.

00:44:26.840 --> 00:44:30.500
And you repeat that
through your whole corpus.

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:36.460
Well, at the end of this,
what the authors realize

00:44:36.460 --> 00:44:39.340
is that the latent
representation

00:44:39.340 --> 00:44:42.620
that you learn in the
middle of the network

00:44:42.620 --> 00:44:50.230
actually is making
sense, as in you

00:44:50.230 --> 00:44:54.500
can compute similarity
measures between

00:44:54.500 --> 00:44:56.540
the learned representations.

00:44:56.540 --> 00:45:01.380
And you see that representations
of similar tokens

00:45:01.380 --> 00:45:04.940
are actually similar.

00:45:04.940 --> 00:45:07.940
So in this example,
so Teddy bear is soft,

00:45:07.940 --> 00:45:09.760
what is Persian poetry to art.

00:45:09.760 --> 00:45:12.260
So another example is
Paris is to France,

00:45:12.260 --> 00:45:14.040
what Berlin is to Germany.

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:16.660
So you have these
nice associations

00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:20.820
that are actually something
that the model learns.

00:45:20.820 --> 00:45:27.920
So that gives you meaningful
word representations.

00:45:27.920 --> 00:45:31.900
So that's a method that
appeared, I believe,

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:33.140
in the early 2010's.

00:45:33.140 --> 00:45:36.560
I believe it was 2013,
if I remember correctly.

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:40.700
So who can tell me what is a
limitation of this approach?

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:48.620
So here, the representation
of the words that we learn--

00:45:51.440 --> 00:45:54.180
we saw that they end
up being meaningful.

00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:59.160
But is there a limitation
to this approach?

00:45:59.160 --> 00:46:00.160
Yeah.

00:46:00.160 --> 00:46:04.760
So one answer is that you would
have trouble if you actually

00:46:04.760 --> 00:46:07.640
are not encountering
something that you have seen

00:46:07.640 --> 00:46:10.300
during your training process.

00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:11.420
So yes, that is right.

00:46:11.420 --> 00:46:14.160
And I think that's right
in actually most cases,

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:16.560
beyond that case in particular.

00:46:16.560 --> 00:46:17.380
So that's one.

00:46:17.380 --> 00:46:20.880
Is there another-- yes,
another limitation?

00:46:20.880 --> 00:46:25.120
So the answer here is
the dimension scales up

00:46:25.120 --> 00:46:28.030
when the vocabulary
size gets bigger.

00:46:28.030 --> 00:46:32.090
So actually, this is a
limitation of your tokenizer

00:46:32.090 --> 00:46:34.890
because the tokenizer is the one
that determines your vocabulary

00:46:34.890 --> 00:46:37.750
size.

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:41.950
But you're right that
this is a limitation.

00:46:41.950 --> 00:46:43.870
But it's not specific
to that approach,

00:46:43.870 --> 00:46:45.690
as in you will
always need to find

00:46:45.690 --> 00:46:50.230
a representation of each
element in your vocabulary.

00:46:50.230 --> 00:46:54.290
So, I mean, it's a
limitation but limitation

00:46:54.290 --> 00:46:57.290
in methods in general.

00:46:57.290 --> 00:46:58.170
Yes.

00:46:58.170 --> 00:46:58.990
Oh, yes.

00:46:58.990 --> 00:47:01.790
So the answer is words can
have multiple meanings.

00:47:01.790 --> 00:47:02.510
So exactly.

00:47:02.510 --> 00:47:10.890
So one limitation here is if
the word, let's say "cute,"

00:47:10.890 --> 00:47:15.650
is in this sentence versus
in another sentence,

00:47:15.650 --> 00:47:18.110
they will both get the
same representation.

00:47:18.110 --> 00:47:24.430
So if you say cute Teddy bear
in a nice way versus someone who

00:47:24.430 --> 00:47:28.308
sarcastically
says, oh, how cute,

00:47:28.308 --> 00:47:30.350
they may mean different
things between the word--

00:47:30.350 --> 00:47:32.350
behind the word "cute."

00:47:32.350 --> 00:47:38.530
So I think a very classic
example is "bank,"

00:47:38.530 --> 00:47:41.110
so river bank versus
going to the bank.

00:47:41.110 --> 00:47:43.990
These are the same words
but different meaning.

00:47:43.990 --> 00:47:45.670
So the problem
with this approach

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:50.230
is that the representation
of this word

00:47:50.230 --> 00:47:53.350
would actually be the same
regardless of the sentence

00:47:53.350 --> 00:47:54.870
that it is in.

00:47:54.870 --> 00:47:56.430
So great point.

00:47:56.430 --> 00:48:02.310
Another limitation here is that
the word order does not matter.

00:48:02.310 --> 00:48:07.970
So let's assume we're saying the
child is hugging the Teddy bear,

00:48:07.970 --> 00:48:10.950
and the Teddy bear
is hugging the child.

00:48:10.950 --> 00:48:15.050
These are the exact same words,
but they mean different things.

00:48:15.050 --> 00:48:18.310
We would get the same
embeddings with this approach

00:48:18.310 --> 00:48:21.390
in both cases.

00:48:21.390 --> 00:48:24.930
So these are all
limitations, which

00:48:24.930 --> 00:48:28.090
is the reason why
this is something

00:48:28.090 --> 00:48:33.410
that people have tried to
resolve through different means.

00:48:33.410 --> 00:48:41.610
One such means is RNNs,
which, as we previously saw,

00:48:41.610 --> 00:48:46.970
was the type of model that
was dominating the models back

00:48:46.970 --> 00:48:49.250
in the 2010's.

00:48:49.250 --> 00:48:54.770
And so here, RNNs, what they
do is that they actually

00:48:54.770 --> 00:48:58.310
have an extra quantity
that they compute,

00:48:58.310 --> 00:49:01.600
which here is
represented with A.

00:49:01.600 --> 00:49:05.090
There is also called
a hidden state

00:49:05.090 --> 00:49:10.570
that tries to encapsulate the
meaning of the sentence seen

00:49:10.570 --> 00:49:12.770
so far.

00:49:12.770 --> 00:49:15.030
So we're going to see
that as an example.

00:49:15.030 --> 00:49:17.070
So let's assume we go
with the same sentence.

00:49:17.070 --> 00:49:18.850
So a cute Teddy bear is reading.

00:49:18.850 --> 00:49:25.420
So here, you would actually
put the word into the RNN.

00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:28.180
It would try to predict
the word "cute."

00:49:28.180 --> 00:49:32.420
But then what it
will do is keep what

00:49:32.420 --> 00:49:36.140
we call a hidden state
that encodes the meaning

00:49:36.140 --> 00:49:39.780
of the sequence seen so far.

00:49:39.780 --> 00:49:44.160
That will be an input
to the next prediction.

00:49:44.160 --> 00:49:49.660
So we'll have "cute" as an input
and then this hidden states

00:49:49.660 --> 00:49:55.980
as an input as well in order to
inform the next representation,

00:49:55.980 --> 00:49:57.860
and so on and so forth.

00:49:57.860 --> 00:50:01.060
So here, what you're
doing is you're not just

00:50:01.060 --> 00:50:06.420
considering embeddings
of tokens on their own.

00:50:06.420 --> 00:50:10.060
You're also considering
the embedding

00:50:10.060 --> 00:50:13.780
of the sequence decoded so far.

00:50:13.780 --> 00:50:19.000
And that allows you to
take into a consideration

00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:23.920
the order of the
words in the sentence.

00:50:23.920 --> 00:50:26.680
And RNNs are
actually models that

00:50:26.680 --> 00:50:29.437
were used in a bunch of
tasks back in the day.

00:50:29.437 --> 00:50:31.020
So, for instance,
for classifications,

00:50:31.020 --> 00:50:34.940
what you would do is
process tokens one at a time

00:50:34.940 --> 00:50:38.040
and then keep a
self-mutating state that

00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:41.320
encodes the meaning of
the sequence seen so far.

00:50:41.320 --> 00:50:44.020
And it would try to
predict, let's say,

00:50:44.020 --> 00:50:47.840
a sentiment in the
sentiment extraction case.

00:50:47.840 --> 00:50:50.580
In the
multi-classification case,

00:50:50.580 --> 00:50:53.040
for instance, if you
wanted to determine

00:50:53.040 --> 00:50:55.800
if a word was a noun
or a verb, then you

00:50:55.800 --> 00:50:59.720
would actually leverage the
representations corresponding

00:50:59.720 --> 00:51:02.200
to each input,
which again, would

00:51:02.200 --> 00:51:07.320
take into consideration
the hidden states or just

00:51:07.320 --> 00:51:08.300
generation.

00:51:11.080 --> 00:51:16.340
But one limitation
of RNNs is that--

00:51:16.340 --> 00:51:16.840
OK.

00:51:16.840 --> 00:51:21.020
It was great you would consider
the order at which the words

00:51:21.020 --> 00:51:24.060
would appear in a sequence.

00:51:24.060 --> 00:51:29.700
But it would have trouble
remembering words that

00:51:29.700 --> 00:51:34.460
happened far in the past,
because what it used to do

00:51:34.460 --> 00:51:38.980
was to encode the
meaning of the sequence

00:51:38.980 --> 00:51:44.340
in one single vector that
kept on being modified.

00:51:44.340 --> 00:51:50.140
So you had this issue of what
we call long-range dependency,

00:51:50.140 --> 00:51:54.140
where if you end up two
sentences after something

00:51:54.140 --> 00:51:56.980
that you have talked
about prior to that

00:51:56.980 --> 00:52:02.220
would have trouble teasing
that out just using

00:52:02.220 --> 00:52:05.940
the hidden state at that point.

00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:08.940
And that can be
problematic, for instance,

00:52:08.940 --> 00:52:12.220
when you refer to something
that happened in the past.

00:52:12.220 --> 00:52:17.110
So for instance, you can say,
oh, my Teddy bear is so cute.

00:52:17.110 --> 00:52:20.270
It is 3 feet tall.

00:52:20.270 --> 00:52:23.710
So "it" refers to something
that is in the past.

00:52:23.710 --> 00:52:27.390
So now let's assume that
"it" I'm using is referring

00:52:27.390 --> 00:52:30.390
to something that
is far in the past.

00:52:30.390 --> 00:52:33.430
Then we have this
problem of actually

00:52:33.430 --> 00:52:36.550
having the right set of
information in the hidden state

00:52:36.550 --> 00:52:40.110
that we're considering.

00:52:40.110 --> 00:52:46.430
So this is the reason why there
were some variations of RNNs.

00:52:46.430 --> 00:52:52.390
One such variation was called
LSTM, Long Short-Term Memory,

00:52:52.390 --> 00:52:55.190
which tried to
address this problem,

00:52:55.190 --> 00:52:58.910
by not only keeping
a hidden state

00:52:58.910 --> 00:53:03.030
but also another state
called the cell state

00:53:03.030 --> 00:53:10.670
that was meant to carry
information for longer.

00:53:10.670 --> 00:53:15.610
But the truth is
even such variations

00:53:15.610 --> 00:53:22.330
had their limitations, which,
again, had the same issue.

00:53:22.330 --> 00:53:27.130
So if we were to just recap
the state of the ways we could

00:53:27.130 --> 00:53:31.010
obtain our embeddings
so far, we saw

00:53:31.010 --> 00:53:34.610
Word2vec, which is a
very simple approach

00:53:34.610 --> 00:53:39.290
to compute
representations of words.

00:53:39.290 --> 00:53:43.230
But the problem is
that if a word is,

00:53:43.230 --> 00:53:46.090
let's say, in a
different sequence, then

00:53:46.090 --> 00:53:47.670
the representation is the same.

00:53:47.670 --> 00:53:49.990
So that is a limitation.

00:53:49.990 --> 00:53:53.810
And also as
mentioned previously,

00:53:53.810 --> 00:53:57.010
the same word can actually
mean different things.

00:53:57.010 --> 00:54:00.370
And so that is
also not conveyed.

00:54:00.370 --> 00:54:03.370
And in the case of
RNNs, we are actually

00:54:03.370 --> 00:54:07.250
addressing the
word order problem

00:54:07.250 --> 00:54:11.110
by having a hidden state that
encapsulates the sequence that

00:54:11.110 --> 00:54:13.750
is seen so far.

00:54:13.750 --> 00:54:17.270
But the problem is that we
have this long-range dependency

00:54:17.270 --> 00:54:18.150
problem.

00:54:18.150 --> 00:54:21.590
That is actually coming
from a deeper problem called

00:54:21.590 --> 00:54:23.590
the vanishing-gradient
problem, which

00:54:23.590 --> 00:54:28.270
is a problem when you
want to backpropagate,

00:54:28.270 --> 00:54:31.150
that just introduces
some limitation.

00:54:31.150 --> 00:54:35.510
And another thing that we
have not mentioned, but RNNs,

00:54:35.510 --> 00:54:40.150
the problem is every time you
want to predict the next token,

00:54:40.150 --> 00:54:43.750
you need to have the
hidden state that

00:54:43.750 --> 00:54:47.230
encapsulates all the things
that you have seen so far.

00:54:47.230 --> 00:54:52.270
So you always have to
just go through tokens one

00:54:52.270 --> 00:54:54.450
at a time in order to
make the next prediction.

00:54:54.450 --> 00:54:56.990
And when it comes to
training such a model,

00:54:56.990 --> 00:54:58.810
it is just extremely slow.

00:55:01.670 --> 00:55:10.490
And it had great results at the
time but not amazing either.

00:55:10.490 --> 00:55:15.410
So for all of these reasons,
in the 2010's, people

00:55:15.410 --> 00:55:17.490
tried different techniques.

00:55:17.490 --> 00:55:22.930
And there is one technique
that really stood out now

00:55:22.930 --> 00:55:27.770
from hindsight, but that
is called attention.

00:55:27.770 --> 00:55:30.930
And the idea is as follows.

00:55:30.930 --> 00:55:35.290
Instead of solely relying
on the hidden state

00:55:35.290 --> 00:55:37.530
to encapsulate the
meaning of the sequence

00:55:37.530 --> 00:55:40.890
that you have seen
so far, the idea

00:55:40.890 --> 00:55:47.930
is to let your model have
direct connections from tokens

00:55:47.930 --> 00:55:51.010
that you have seen in the past.

00:55:51.010 --> 00:55:54.050
So in other words,
we're not saying

00:55:54.050 --> 00:55:56.170
only rely on the
hidden state to know

00:55:56.170 --> 00:55:57.970
what has happened in the past.

00:55:57.970 --> 00:56:02.530
We're saying you can also have
direct connections to tokens

00:56:02.530 --> 00:56:04.970
that you have seen
in the past in order

00:56:04.970 --> 00:56:08.540
to predict the next token.

00:56:08.540 --> 00:56:11.580
So in the translation example,
so let's assume you want

00:56:11.580 --> 00:56:15.340
to translate in French, "A
cute Teddy bear is reading."

00:56:15.340 --> 00:56:16.620
And let's assume you say--

00:56:16.620 --> 00:56:18.500
[SPEAKING FRENCH]

00:56:18.500 --> 00:56:20.940
And you want to
predict the next word.

00:56:20.940 --> 00:56:26.940
So intuitively, in order
to predict the next word,

00:56:26.940 --> 00:56:29.620
it would be amazing to
know what the word that you

00:56:29.620 --> 00:56:32.460
want to translate is.

00:56:32.460 --> 00:56:34.980
So in the traditional
RNN case, you

00:56:34.980 --> 00:56:38.740
would not have access to that--
direct access to that word.

00:56:38.740 --> 00:56:42.380
You would only have access
to it via the hidden state.

00:56:42.380 --> 00:56:46.300
So here, the idea is to
have a direct connection

00:56:46.300 --> 00:56:51.660
to previous tokens and let the
model learn which ones matter.

00:56:51.660 --> 00:56:54.460
And here, for the prediction,
to rely on the ones

00:56:54.460 --> 00:57:00.380
that matter in order
to make the prediction.

00:57:00.380 --> 00:57:04.400
So this is the
concept of attention.

00:57:04.400 --> 00:57:06.840
The concept of attention
is letting your model

00:57:06.840 --> 00:57:11.240
draw direct connections
from a given token

00:57:11.240 --> 00:57:13.540
to some tokens in the past.

00:57:16.200 --> 00:57:21.360
And this concept
is very important

00:57:21.360 --> 00:57:25.220
because it is at the foundation
of what we're going to see now.

00:57:27.840 --> 00:57:33.120
So I mentioned previously that
there was this paper called

00:57:33.120 --> 00:57:36.760
"Attention is All You Need"
that came out in 2017.

00:57:36.760 --> 00:57:39.680
That was a turning
point because people

00:57:39.680 --> 00:57:41.820
saw that it was very scalable.

00:57:41.820 --> 00:57:45.440
It was giving you
amazing results.

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:50.160
Well, this paper
relies on the concept

00:57:50.160 --> 00:57:57.360
of the self-attention, which
is attention but on steroids.

00:57:57.360 --> 00:58:01.760
And we'll go through exactly
what we mean by that.

00:58:01.760 --> 00:58:06.380
So let's assume you have the
sequence "A cute Teddy bear is

00:58:06.380 --> 00:58:08.020
reading."

00:58:08.020 --> 00:58:10.700
So now the idea is
to not say that we're

00:58:10.700 --> 00:58:12.820
going to compute the
representation of a given

00:58:12.820 --> 00:58:19.220
word as a function of the
hidden state that encapsulates

00:58:19.220 --> 00:58:23.240
the meaning of the sequence
so far and the word itself.

00:58:23.240 --> 00:58:24.280
We're not saying that.

00:58:24.280 --> 00:58:26.940
Now what we're saying
is that in order

00:58:26.940 --> 00:58:29.820
to compute the representation
of a given token,

00:58:29.820 --> 00:58:34.300
we can actually do that by
expressing that representation

00:58:34.300 --> 00:58:39.740
as a function of all the
other tokens in that sequence.

00:58:39.740 --> 00:58:41.260
Sorry?

00:58:41.260 --> 00:58:43.820
So I think you raise an
important point, which

00:58:43.820 --> 00:58:47.180
is, how do you know
the order of words

00:58:47.180 --> 00:58:49.940
if you just let everyone
interact to everyone, in case

00:58:49.940 --> 00:58:51.660
that was your question.

00:58:51.660 --> 00:58:56.700
So if you let a token interact
with all other tokens,

00:58:56.700 --> 00:59:02.270
you actually lose the
concept of word order.

00:59:02.270 --> 00:59:04.890
And it is actually something
that is addressed later on.

00:59:04.890 --> 00:59:07.310
So we're going to
exactly see that part.

00:59:07.310 --> 00:59:09.990
But here, you
actually do not know

00:59:09.990 --> 00:59:14.150
where tokens are because you
have a direct connection.

00:59:14.150 --> 00:59:16.330
In this very vanilla
representation,

00:59:16.330 --> 00:59:17.930
you don't know where tokens are.

00:59:17.930 --> 00:59:22.870
So we're going to see exactly
how you fix that issue.

00:59:22.870 --> 00:59:24.970
But does the idea make sense?

00:59:24.970 --> 00:59:27.230
The idea that in
order to compute

00:59:27.230 --> 00:59:30.310
a representation of
a token, you actually

00:59:30.310 --> 00:59:32.750
want to express that
as a function of all

00:59:32.750 --> 00:59:34.210
the other tokens
in the sequence,

00:59:34.210 --> 00:59:35.730
does this idea make sense?

00:59:40.310 --> 00:59:40.910
Roughly?

00:59:43.870 --> 00:59:45.250
Yeah, I'll take that as a yes.

00:59:45.250 --> 00:59:47.050
So we're going to
go through examples.

00:59:47.050 --> 00:59:49.610
And actually, Shervine has
a detailed example later on.

00:59:49.610 --> 00:59:53.190
So hopefully that will
make a bit more sense.

00:59:53.190 --> 00:59:58.610
There is a set of notations
that people use that we're

00:59:58.610 --> 01:00:00.330
going to introduce now.

01:00:00.330 --> 01:00:07.130
So these notations are
queries, keys, and values,

01:00:07.130 --> 01:00:16.690
Q, K, and V. So the
query is the entity

01:00:16.690 --> 01:00:22.730
that is trying to determine
what a given token is about.

01:00:22.730 --> 01:00:30.010
And the idea is it wants to find
tokens that are useful in order

01:00:30.010 --> 01:00:32.770
to construct a meaningful
representation of the token

01:00:32.770 --> 01:00:34.670
that it is responsible for.

01:00:34.670 --> 01:00:37.930
So the query for Teddy
bear, what it tries to do

01:00:37.930 --> 01:00:40.850
is to find other
tokens that is relevant

01:00:40.850 --> 01:00:45.890
in order to compute a meaningful
representation of that token.

01:00:45.890 --> 01:00:50.890
So what it will do is see
how similar that quantity

01:00:50.890 --> 01:00:56.430
is to keys of the sequence
that are associated

01:00:56.430 --> 01:01:00.150
with each token in the sequence.

01:01:00.150 --> 01:01:03.870
And once it determines
the magnitude at which it

01:01:03.870 --> 01:01:09.310
is similar to each token,
we will associate a value

01:01:09.310 --> 01:01:12.870
to each of these tokens.

01:01:12.870 --> 01:01:15.910
So in other words, we want
to express the representation

01:01:15.910 --> 01:01:19.350
of Teddy bear as a function
of all the other tokens

01:01:19.350 --> 01:01:20.510
in the sequence.

01:01:20.510 --> 01:01:23.750
So what we will do is first
determine the weights.

01:01:23.750 --> 01:01:25.130
So it's a weighted average.

01:01:25.130 --> 01:01:25.970
We want the weights.

01:01:25.970 --> 01:01:29.550
The weights are determined by
how similar the query for Teddy

01:01:29.550 --> 01:01:33.270
bear is to each key.

01:01:33.270 --> 01:01:38.030
And then the actual value
will be just the value vector

01:01:38.030 --> 01:01:42.290
corresponding to each token.

01:01:42.290 --> 01:01:44.970
So it will be something
like-- if I were to write it,

01:01:44.970 --> 01:01:50.270
so it's like the sum
of something that

01:01:50.270 --> 01:01:52.410
is representing the similarity.

01:01:52.410 --> 01:01:59.960
So Q and Ki, Vi.

01:01:59.960 --> 01:02:01.760
So this is your query.

01:02:01.760 --> 01:02:06.480
You're trying to determine
how similar your query is

01:02:06.480 --> 01:02:09.940
to all the other keys with
some similarity operations.

01:02:09.940 --> 01:02:12.400
So here's the dot
product as an example.

01:02:12.400 --> 01:02:13.460
And you associate that.

01:02:13.460 --> 01:02:15.480
So this is a scalar.

01:02:15.480 --> 01:02:17.760
This is a scalar.

01:02:17.760 --> 01:02:22.340
And you associate that with
Vi which is also a vector.

01:02:25.640 --> 01:02:28.973
So this may seem a
little bit abstract,

01:02:28.973 --> 01:02:30.140
so I'll give you an example.

01:02:30.140 --> 01:02:34.160
So let's assume we want to know
what best describes the Teddy

01:02:34.160 --> 01:02:35.080
bear.

01:02:35.080 --> 01:02:37.240
The query can be
asking itself, OK,

01:02:37.240 --> 01:02:39.560
what best describes
a Teddy bear?

01:02:39.560 --> 01:02:40.860
And you look at all the tokens.

01:02:40.860 --> 01:02:45.680
You're like, oh, "cute" actually
describes the Teddy bear pretty

01:02:45.680 --> 01:02:46.960
well.

01:02:46.960 --> 01:02:50.000
So maybe the dot
product between cute,

01:02:50.000 --> 01:02:56.260
Teddy bear, and K cute
is going to be high.

01:02:56.260 --> 01:02:58.500
So the representation
that you compute

01:02:58.500 --> 01:03:01.860
for Teddy bear is actually
something that will depend

01:03:01.860 --> 01:03:04.080
heavily on cute, let's assume.

01:03:06.660 --> 01:03:10.080
So these are the concepts
of query key value.

01:03:10.080 --> 01:03:12.740
Now the next question is,
how do you obtain those?

01:03:12.740 --> 01:03:16.300
How do you obtain
queries key values?

01:03:16.300 --> 01:03:20.460
So these are actually
quantities that you can

01:03:20.460 --> 01:03:25.220
learn with projection matrices.

01:03:25.220 --> 01:03:28.620
So you initially
have some input token

01:03:28.620 --> 01:03:33.220
that represents each token.

01:03:33.220 --> 01:03:37.740
And what you do is you
take that representation,

01:03:37.740 --> 01:03:45.860
and you project it on the space
of queries, of keys, of values

01:03:45.860 --> 01:03:49.780
through some
projection matrices.

01:03:49.780 --> 01:03:52.120
And then you use
these quantities

01:03:52.120 --> 01:04:00.440
in order to compute meaningful
representations of tokens.

01:04:00.440 --> 01:04:02.272
Yes.

01:04:02.272 --> 01:04:02.980
Amazing question.

01:04:02.980 --> 01:04:04.420
So the question
is, what is the T?

01:04:04.420 --> 01:04:07.340
So T is actually just to
be mathematically rigorous.

01:04:07.340 --> 01:04:09.840
So T is transpose.

01:04:09.840 --> 01:04:17.620
So it's just that this, you can
also write it as QK transpose.

01:04:25.400 --> 01:04:28.320
Any other question?

01:04:28.320 --> 01:04:30.220
So we have a detailed
example later.

01:04:30.220 --> 01:04:36.400
So in case that roughly makes
sense, I think that's great.

01:04:36.400 --> 01:04:40.240
We'll see that in a second.

01:04:40.240 --> 01:04:43.080
So it just turns out
that what I described

01:04:43.080 --> 01:04:46.680
is something that is
very nicely-- something

01:04:46.680 --> 01:04:52.180
you can express in mathematical
terms with matrices.

01:04:52.180 --> 01:04:55.700
So in particular, the
operation I just mentioned

01:04:55.700 --> 01:04:57.500
is something that
can be expressed

01:04:57.500 --> 01:05:02.660
as the softmax of the
query and the keys.

01:05:02.660 --> 01:05:08.180
And you scale that by some
factor times the values.

01:05:08.180 --> 01:05:13.980
And actually, I maybe have
enough time for you to say why.

01:05:13.980 --> 01:05:17.100
So let's assume you have--

01:05:17.100 --> 01:05:18.820
so the softmax is
just a way for you

01:05:18.820 --> 01:05:24.000
to get the sum of the
coefficients being equal to 1.

01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:25.900
So that's roughly what it does.

01:05:25.900 --> 01:05:32.660
But the question is, well, if
you have query key transpose V--

01:05:32.660 --> 01:05:38.660
I mean, more or less the
softmax and the scaling,

01:05:38.660 --> 01:05:42.740
why does it give you the
thing that we saw above?

01:05:42.740 --> 01:05:48.350
Well, let's assume that
your query matrix has

01:05:48.350 --> 01:05:54.110
all your queries, so Q1, Qn.

01:05:54.110 --> 01:05:57.730
So 1 is the first
index of your sequence.

01:05:57.730 --> 01:05:59.550
So let's assume in
the previous slides

01:05:59.550 --> 01:06:04.350
that we say the first index
is A. Let's say A is 1.

01:06:04.350 --> 01:06:08.550
And then the dot
is n, let's assume.

01:06:08.550 --> 01:06:16.870
So we have the query for A up
until the query for the dot.

01:06:16.870 --> 01:06:22.430
And you do the same for K and V.

01:06:22.430 --> 01:06:30.870
So if you multiply this query
matrix times Q transpose,

01:06:30.870 --> 01:06:38.570
what you get is
K1, like this, Kn.

01:06:43.190 --> 01:06:49.090
And so this is where you're
glad some linear algebra.

01:06:49.090 --> 01:06:50.970
So matrix
multiplication-- so you

01:06:50.970 --> 01:07:01.090
do this times this, which
is the dot product of Q1 K1.

01:07:01.090 --> 01:07:04.430
And then second
element is Q1 K2.

01:07:11.650 --> 01:07:16.250
So if you repeat this, you will
have the matrix of the QIKJ.

01:07:19.970 --> 01:07:22.430
I'm going to maybe
write that up up there.

01:07:33.010 --> 01:07:37.970
So I'm just going to write
exactly what is above.

01:07:37.970 --> 01:07:38.950
So you have--

01:07:47.270 --> 01:07:51.750
Q1 K1.

01:07:51.750 --> 01:07:55.130
Up until Q1 Kn.

01:07:58.670 --> 01:08:00.090
And then you repeat that here.

01:08:00.090 --> 01:08:00.890
You put that here.

01:08:00.890 --> 01:08:02.110
You repeat that here.

01:08:02.110 --> 01:08:22.270
Now if you add a V in here,
then what you obtain is V1 Vn.

01:08:22.270 --> 01:08:26.550
So again, you do your
matrix multiplication.

01:08:26.550 --> 01:08:29.710
And here, what you get is--

01:08:29.710 --> 01:08:33.350
this times this plus this times
this plus this times this,

01:08:33.350 --> 01:08:34.670
up until here.

01:08:34.670 --> 01:08:46.020
So you have the sum of Q1 Ki Vi.

01:08:48.600 --> 01:08:58.760
And the same up until
the sum of Qn, Ki, Vi.

01:09:01.640 --> 01:09:06.080
So long story
short, I just wanted

01:09:06.080 --> 01:09:13.120
to say that the fact
of expressing something

01:09:13.120 --> 01:09:18.222
as a function of this similarity
operation and the values

01:09:18.222 --> 01:09:19.680
is something that
you can represent

01:09:19.680 --> 01:09:21.180
very well with matrices.

01:09:23.960 --> 01:09:26.920
And you will see this
formula quite often.

01:09:26.920 --> 01:09:29.439
So I believe there was a
question on the midterm.

01:09:29.439 --> 01:09:35.080
So hint, this formula
can be important.

01:09:35.080 --> 01:09:40.040
And so now that we know how
self-attention is defined,

01:09:40.040 --> 01:09:43.220
how self-attention works,
now we're going to, hopefully

01:09:43.220 --> 01:09:48.859
in the next 17 minutes, see
how self-attention is used

01:09:48.859 --> 01:09:52.439
in the transformer
architecture, which is a--

01:09:52.439 --> 01:09:55.900
which is the architecture that
was introduced in the "Attention

01:09:55.900 --> 01:09:58.140
is All You Need" paper.

01:09:58.140 --> 01:10:03.180
So why is it called
"Attention is All You Need?"

01:10:03.180 --> 01:10:10.800
Well, the authors at the time,
most models, were recurrent.

01:10:10.800 --> 01:10:12.420
They were based on RNN.

01:10:12.420 --> 01:10:16.740
So they used to compute
things in a recurrent fashion.

01:10:16.740 --> 01:10:18.880
But here, what we're
saying is actually,

01:10:18.880 --> 01:10:25.700
we don't care about keeping
a hidden state by recurring

01:10:25.700 --> 01:10:27.160
through tokens.

01:10:27.160 --> 01:10:28.780
We're not doing that.

01:10:28.780 --> 01:10:32.300
What we're doing instead
is letting tokens

01:10:32.300 --> 01:10:39.200
interact with all tokens through
direct connections, a.k.a.

01:10:39.200 --> 01:10:44.200
through self-attention, which
is why attention is all you

01:10:44.200 --> 01:10:46.400
Need.

01:10:46.400 --> 01:10:48.640
So this paper was
released in the context

01:10:48.640 --> 01:10:51.200
of machine translation.

01:10:51.200 --> 01:10:54.160
So in this context, we're
interested in translating

01:10:54.160 --> 01:10:56.760
a source text, let's
say in English, over

01:10:56.760 --> 01:11:00.200
to another language,
let's say in French.

01:11:00.200 --> 01:11:05.960
And what we do is we have two
components in the transformer.

01:11:05.960 --> 01:11:10.240
On the left, we have
what we call an encoder.

01:11:10.240 --> 01:11:14.040
And the role of the
encoder is to compute

01:11:14.040 --> 01:11:17.480
meaningful representations
for the source

01:11:17.480 --> 01:11:21.440
text that is fed as input.

01:11:21.440 --> 01:11:23.840
And we have a second
part of the transformer

01:11:23.840 --> 01:11:26.640
which is called the decoder.

01:11:26.640 --> 01:11:29.760
And the role of the
decoder is to use

01:11:29.760 --> 01:11:33.840
the meaningful representations
from the encoder

01:11:33.840 --> 01:11:37.170
along with some
internal embeddings

01:11:37.170 --> 01:11:42.630
that it is computing in order
to generate the translation.

01:11:45.370 --> 01:11:49.530
So that is the context in which
the transformer was released,

01:11:49.530 --> 01:11:51.770
and it performed great.

01:11:51.770 --> 01:11:55.810
And it is at the foundation
of modern-day LLMs, which

01:11:55.810 --> 01:11:58.030
is the reason why
we're focusing on this.

01:11:58.030 --> 01:12:00.490
So that's the reason
why we're here.

01:12:00.490 --> 01:12:04.530
The reason why I wrote these
formulas on the Blackboard

01:12:04.530 --> 01:12:06.850
is because I think
this is probably

01:12:06.850 --> 01:12:10.770
the most important formula
when it comes to transformers.

01:12:10.770 --> 01:12:16.770
So just visualizing this in
terms of matrix multiplies

01:12:16.770 --> 01:12:19.710
is very important.

01:12:19.710 --> 01:12:23.610
So what we will do now is
walk through each component

01:12:23.610 --> 01:12:28.432
of the transformer and see
how this all comes together.

01:12:28.432 --> 01:12:29.890
And I'm looking at
you with respect

01:12:29.890 --> 01:12:31.850
to the position embeddings.

01:12:31.850 --> 01:12:34.030
We're going to talk
about this now.

01:12:34.030 --> 01:12:39.590
So for machine
translation, what you

01:12:39.590 --> 01:12:44.990
get as input is a text
in a source language.

01:12:44.990 --> 01:12:47.230
And your task is to
translate that source

01:12:47.230 --> 01:12:50.990
language, that text
in the source language

01:12:50.990 --> 01:12:53.910
into a target language.

01:12:53.910 --> 01:12:57.870
So your first task
is to understand

01:12:57.870 --> 01:13:00.750
what your source text is about.

01:13:00.750 --> 01:13:04.030
So here, you have
an input embedding

01:13:04.030 --> 01:13:08.430
that is tasked with just
converting this input

01:13:08.430 --> 01:13:13.510
text into tokens and then
associating each of these tokens

01:13:13.510 --> 01:13:16.310
with some embedding
that you can learn.

01:13:16.310 --> 01:13:20.630
This embedding is only
with respect to each token.

01:13:20.630 --> 01:13:24.070
It is not context
aware in any way.

01:13:24.070 --> 01:13:28.470
And what you do is you
add an embedding that

01:13:28.470 --> 01:13:31.130
is associated with the
position that the token is

01:13:31.130 --> 01:13:33.930
in the sequence.

01:13:33.930 --> 01:13:39.290
And this is where we know
where tokens are located.

01:13:39.290 --> 01:13:42.970
So we add the token
embedding and the position

01:13:42.970 --> 01:13:46.290
embedding together.

01:13:46.290 --> 01:13:48.250
And so the question
you may have is, well,

01:13:48.250 --> 01:13:50.650
how do you obtain the
position embedding?

01:13:50.650 --> 01:13:52.730
So you have different ways.

01:13:52.730 --> 01:13:56.930
The ways people-- like
the authors have tried

01:13:56.930 --> 01:13:58.650
is actually two ways.

01:13:58.650 --> 01:14:03.233
One way is to learn
the embedding.

01:14:03.233 --> 01:14:04.650
The second way is
to actually have

01:14:04.650 --> 01:14:11.090
something that is a function
of just different frequencies.

01:14:11.090 --> 01:14:13.490
So there are some pros and cons.

01:14:13.490 --> 01:14:16.530
But I just want to--
so I think the learning

01:14:16.530 --> 01:14:18.570
way is very natural.

01:14:18.570 --> 01:14:23.690
But a clear con is if you have
an input text that is longer

01:14:23.690 --> 01:14:27.130
than what you have learned at
training time-- so if you have,

01:14:27.130 --> 01:14:31.780
let's say 1,000 tokens as
input but you already--

01:14:31.780 --> 01:14:35.540
you only have learned positions,
like embeddings for positions,

01:14:35.540 --> 01:14:38.480
up to, let's say 500,
then you're out of luck.

01:14:38.480 --> 01:14:41.240
You cannot represent
positions greater than that,

01:14:41.240 --> 01:14:46.620
which is why the arbitrary,
predefined representation can be

01:14:46.620 --> 01:14:48.420
useful.

01:14:48.420 --> 01:14:51.620
And I just want
to illustrate why

01:14:51.620 --> 01:14:56.420
choosing different frequencies
could be a good way to represent

01:14:56.420 --> 01:14:59.300
something like the position.

01:14:59.300 --> 01:15:01.900
So you may have a
watch, and you may

01:15:01.900 --> 01:15:04.900
notice that you
represent the time with,

01:15:04.900 --> 01:15:11.320
let's say an hour thingy,
with the minutes, seconds.

01:15:11.320 --> 01:15:14.340
They all turn at
different frequencies.

01:15:14.340 --> 01:15:17.220
So if you want to read
the time, so for 4:49.

01:15:17.220 --> 01:15:22.280
It's 4, which is the hour piece.

01:15:22.280 --> 01:15:25.180
And then you have the minutes,
and then you have the seconds.

01:15:25.180 --> 01:15:28.600
And each of them they turn
at different frequencies.

01:15:28.600 --> 01:15:30.600
And if you put
them together, you

01:15:30.600 --> 01:15:34.440
can exactly know
what time it is.

01:15:34.440 --> 01:15:37.280
So this is the
idea behind having

01:15:37.280 --> 01:15:41.860
a vector of elements that vary
with different frequencies.

01:15:41.860 --> 01:15:45.760
So you can obtain that with
cosine and sine functions.

01:15:45.760 --> 01:15:48.720
And this is what
people use here.

01:15:48.720 --> 01:15:51.000
So the good thing with
these representations

01:15:51.000 --> 01:15:59.820
is that positions that are close
to one another are very similar.

01:15:59.820 --> 01:16:02.660
And the ones that are far from
one another are dissimilar.

01:16:02.660 --> 01:16:05.280
So this is one nice property.

01:16:05.280 --> 01:16:08.520
So this is one way of
representing the positions.

01:16:08.520 --> 01:16:10.220
Anyway, maybe I
digress too much.

01:16:10.220 --> 01:16:12.740
We're going to talk more
about position next time.

01:16:12.740 --> 01:16:18.320
But long story short, we
can represent tokens along

01:16:18.320 --> 01:16:20.040
with their positions.

01:16:20.040 --> 01:16:22.640
So at this stage,
what we did was

01:16:22.640 --> 01:16:27.580
add the token embedding
and the position embedding.

01:16:27.580 --> 01:16:30.940
And here, we come
into the encoder.

01:16:30.940 --> 01:16:34.260
So the encoder is the component
of the transformer which

01:16:34.260 --> 01:16:37.740
is tasked with computing
meaningful representations

01:16:37.740 --> 01:16:39.620
of the tokens.

01:16:39.620 --> 01:16:43.700
So here, we have one
very important type

01:16:43.700 --> 01:16:47.207
of layer called the
attention layer.

01:16:47.207 --> 01:16:49.040
It's actually called a
self-attention layer.

01:16:49.040 --> 01:16:51.020
So a self-attention
layer, what does it do?

01:16:51.020 --> 01:16:56.260
What it does is
computes representations

01:16:56.260 --> 01:17:01.060
of tokens as a function
of all other tokens.

01:17:01.060 --> 01:17:06.700
And this is where this query
key value thing happens.

01:17:06.700 --> 01:17:08.700
So you're going to see
that in the example

01:17:08.700 --> 01:17:10.200
that Shervine will
walk you through.

01:17:10.200 --> 01:17:15.380
But the idea is to learn
projection matrices that

01:17:15.380 --> 01:17:21.420
will allow you to project your
tokens into a space of queries,

01:17:21.420 --> 01:17:25.240
keys, and values,
that will then allow

01:17:25.240 --> 01:17:29.380
them to interact through
the formula that we saw.

01:17:32.680 --> 01:17:35.880
So that allows you to
represent your tokens

01:17:35.880 --> 01:17:38.560
as a function of others.

01:17:38.560 --> 01:17:42.560
And then you have
another layer called

01:17:42.560 --> 01:17:45.480
the feedforward
neural network, which

01:17:45.480 --> 01:17:52.000
is a way for your architecture
to project representations

01:17:52.000 --> 01:17:57.760
into spaces of higher dimension,
along with some activation

01:17:57.760 --> 01:18:00.780
function, which allows you
to learn nonlinearities.

01:18:04.320 --> 01:18:05.800
So you go through the encoder.

01:18:05.800 --> 01:18:08.440
At the tip of the
encoder, what you get

01:18:08.440 --> 01:18:12.600
is a very meaningful
representation per token.

01:18:12.600 --> 01:18:18.400
That is a function of all
other tokens in the sequence.

01:18:18.400 --> 01:18:23.570
So what you do is now, when
you want to translate the text,

01:18:23.570 --> 01:18:25.650
so you take your
special token that we

01:18:25.650 --> 01:18:28.890
saw in the very beginning,
end of sequence-- sorry,

01:18:28.890 --> 01:18:30.210
beginning of sequence.

01:18:30.210 --> 01:18:31.610
So you tell your
model, well, now

01:18:31.610 --> 01:18:33.770
I want to start the generation.

01:18:33.770 --> 01:18:39.530
So you input begin of sequence
as an input to the decoder.

01:18:39.530 --> 01:18:41.890
You add the position
encoding that

01:18:41.890 --> 01:18:44.410
is associated with
that token, which

01:18:44.410 --> 01:18:46.970
is the first element
in the sequence.

01:18:46.970 --> 01:18:56.770
And what you do is you enter the
decoder, which does two things.

01:18:56.770 --> 01:19:04.570
The first thing is it also has
an self-attention mechanism

01:19:04.570 --> 01:19:09.050
with respect to the token
itself and all the tokens that

01:19:09.050 --> 01:19:12.050
was generated so far.

01:19:12.050 --> 01:19:17.730
So it tries to express each
token as a function of itself

01:19:17.730 --> 01:19:20.750
and all the tokens that
were generated so far.

01:19:20.750 --> 01:19:22.810
So in the beginning
of sequence case,

01:19:22.810 --> 01:19:24.730
it's the only token that
was generated so far.

01:19:24.730 --> 01:19:26.310
So that's it.

01:19:26.310 --> 01:19:31.410
And then you have a
second attention layer,

01:19:31.410 --> 01:19:36.470
which is a different kind,
where you're actually

01:19:36.470 --> 01:19:41.950
letting the representations from
tokens that were generated so

01:19:41.950 --> 01:19:48.230
far and let them interact
with tokens from the source,

01:19:48.230 --> 01:19:52.150
from your input source,
from the source language.

01:19:52.150 --> 01:19:54.790
So I'm not sure if you can
see in the illustration,

01:19:54.790 --> 01:19:59.530
you have one arrow which is
from what was generated so far.

01:19:59.530 --> 01:20:03.590
And then you have two arrows
coming from the encoder.

01:20:03.590 --> 01:20:09.390
So the one arrow from the
generator so far is the query.

01:20:09.390 --> 01:20:12.950
And the key and the value
are coming from the encoder

01:20:12.950 --> 01:20:15.710
because you're
asking yourself, how

01:20:15.710 --> 01:20:19.290
can I express this token
as a function of all

01:20:19.290 --> 01:20:24.090
the tokens in the input text?

01:20:24.090 --> 01:20:26.090
By the way, does
that make sense?

01:20:26.090 --> 01:20:27.410
Yeah.

01:20:27.410 --> 01:20:28.055
OK, great.

01:20:28.055 --> 01:20:28.930
And then you do that.

01:20:28.930 --> 01:20:30.530
And then you have
another layer that's

01:20:30.530 --> 01:20:32.530
called the feedforward
neural network that

01:20:32.530 --> 01:20:39.890
again, allows your model to
learn ways to project your data.

01:20:39.890 --> 01:20:45.010
And then at the end, you get a
very meaningful representation

01:20:45.010 --> 01:20:48.515
of the word that the token
that you passed as inputs.

01:20:48.515 --> 01:20:50.390
So here, in this case,
in the very beginning,

01:20:50.390 --> 01:20:52.450
you have beginning of sequence.

01:20:52.450 --> 01:20:55.530
And at the end of
the decoder, you

01:20:55.530 --> 01:21:00.130
have a very meaningful
representation of that token.

01:21:00.130 --> 01:21:03.130
So what you do is you then
project that representation

01:21:03.130 --> 01:21:07.530
on the space of vocabularies.

01:21:07.530 --> 01:21:13.050
And you get probabilities that
correspond to what you think

01:21:13.050 --> 01:21:14.190
is the next token.

01:21:17.340 --> 01:21:22.340
And so here, translation
is just figuring out

01:21:22.340 --> 01:21:23.332
what the next token is.

01:21:23.332 --> 01:21:25.040
So you figure out what
the next token is.

01:21:25.040 --> 01:21:27.780
And then you choose
what that next token

01:21:27.780 --> 01:21:29.620
is based on the probabilities.

01:21:29.620 --> 01:21:33.320
And then you repeat that
in an autoregressive way.

01:21:33.320 --> 01:21:37.720
So you put that new decoded
token as input of the decoder.

01:21:37.720 --> 01:21:39.640
And you repeat exactly
what I mentioned.

01:21:42.300 --> 01:21:46.460
So we have five minutes.

01:21:46.460 --> 01:21:48.860
I have some tricks
to tell you, but I

01:21:48.860 --> 01:21:50.868
want to make sure we're
all on the same page.

01:21:50.868 --> 01:21:52.660
So I'm just going to
open the floor in case

01:21:52.660 --> 01:21:54.840
there is any questions so far.

01:21:54.840 --> 01:21:57.020
We've seen a lot of
things, by the way.

01:21:57.020 --> 01:21:59.900
And this first
lecture is probably

01:21:59.900 --> 01:22:04.380
one of the most difficult in
terms of number of concepts

01:22:04.380 --> 01:22:08.780
we see, so completely normal if
you feel like we've seen a lot.

01:22:08.780 --> 01:22:12.240
Any questions on this?

01:22:12.240 --> 01:22:13.920
Yep.

01:22:13.920 --> 01:22:16.280
So the question is-- there
are multiple arrows merging

01:22:16.280 --> 01:22:17.280
into one.

01:22:17.280 --> 01:22:19.100
So it's because we have
not seen it so far.

01:22:19.100 --> 01:22:21.960
So this is a trick that
actually we can see now.

01:22:21.960 --> 01:22:26.040
So you may be referring to this
one, the residual connections.

01:22:26.040 --> 01:22:31.640
So this is actually a trick to
help your model learn better.

01:22:31.640 --> 01:22:34.000
So these residual
connections, they're

01:22:34.000 --> 01:22:36.840
very useful in
architectures that

01:22:36.840 --> 01:22:39.400
are deep because
they allow your model

01:22:39.400 --> 01:22:44.040
to pass in features directly
instead of having them

01:22:44.040 --> 01:22:48.920
be modified by some sublayer.

01:22:48.920 --> 01:22:53.400
So one way I like to think about
this is instead of thinking

01:22:53.400 --> 01:22:59.900
about each layer as something
that modifies the whole input--

01:22:59.900 --> 01:23:05.220
so instead of saying my layer
is just changing the input--

01:23:05.220 --> 01:23:09.160
so let's assume your input is--

01:23:09.160 --> 01:23:13.740
let's assume your input is x.

01:23:13.740 --> 01:23:15.980
Instead of saying that
the output of a layer

01:23:15.980 --> 01:23:19.580
is some function of
x, what you're saying

01:23:19.580 --> 01:23:26.960
is the output of your layer
is some function of x plus x.

01:23:26.960 --> 01:23:28.460
And the way I like
to think about it

01:23:28.460 --> 01:23:34.660
is it's as if you were taking
x and modifying it, as opposed

01:23:34.660 --> 01:23:38.860
to changing completely x.

01:23:38.860 --> 01:23:47.860
So in practice, doing that
helps with back propagation

01:23:47.860 --> 01:23:52.060
and allows your model
to learn better.

01:23:52.060 --> 01:23:54.160
So this is what
this arrow is about.

01:23:54.160 --> 01:23:55.420
Yeah.

01:23:55.420 --> 01:23:57.660
So speaking of the tricks,
I have some other tricks

01:23:57.660 --> 01:23:58.440
to tell you.

01:23:58.440 --> 01:24:03.180
So one other is the fact of
normalizing the activations

01:24:03.180 --> 01:24:06.660
with what we call
layer normalization.

01:24:06.660 --> 01:24:11.110
So it also helps
with convergence.

01:24:11.110 --> 01:24:14.350
You may have seen
the word "masked"

01:24:14.350 --> 01:24:16.410
self-attention in the decoder.

01:24:16.410 --> 01:24:20.470
And the reason why it's masked
is because you want your decoder

01:24:20.470 --> 01:24:26.110
tokens to only interact with
tokens you have decoded so far,

01:24:26.110 --> 01:24:29.850
because you cannot interact with
tokens you have not decoded yet.

01:24:32.430 --> 01:24:35.890
Multi-head attention-- so what
I described with QKV, well,

01:24:35.890 --> 01:24:39.090
you can do it with
in different ways.

01:24:39.090 --> 01:24:42.230
You can learn
different projections.

01:24:42.230 --> 01:24:46.350
So for those of us who also are
familiar with Vision-related

01:24:46.350 --> 01:24:47.870
models, who knows--

01:24:47.870 --> 01:24:51.070
who has a Vision
background here?

01:24:51.070 --> 01:24:53.270
1, 2, OK.

01:24:53.270 --> 01:24:56.110
So in case you
know-- when it comes

01:24:56.110 --> 01:24:58.990
to convolutional
neural networks,

01:24:58.990 --> 01:25:02.430
when you do the
convolution operation,

01:25:02.430 --> 01:25:04.710
you can use several
filters, which

01:25:04.710 --> 01:25:08.810
are different ways of
convoluting on your inputs.

01:25:08.810 --> 01:25:11.650
So having different
attention heads is also a way

01:25:11.650 --> 01:25:13.810
for your model to learn--

01:25:13.810 --> 01:25:16.850
to have different ways
to learn the same thing

01:25:16.850 --> 01:25:19.410
through different
projection matrices.

01:25:19.410 --> 01:25:25.090
And so you typically
have h such heads.

01:25:25.090 --> 01:25:29.330
So another trick, dropout--
who has heard of dropout?

01:25:29.330 --> 01:25:30.190
OK, great.

01:25:30.190 --> 01:25:33.810
Yeah, this is an
idea that is not

01:25:33.810 --> 01:25:37.430
just applied in the
transformer but widely used.

01:25:37.430 --> 01:25:41.450
So that one is a technique
for which at training time,

01:25:41.450 --> 01:25:45.050
you intentionally
drop some units

01:25:45.050 --> 01:25:49.210
with some probability
in order for you

01:25:49.210 --> 01:25:54.810
to have your model to not rely
too much on some features.

01:25:54.810 --> 01:26:00.250
And that allows your model
to generalize better.

01:26:00.250 --> 01:26:01.710
And then what do I have?

01:26:01.710 --> 01:26:04.310
Yeah, label smoothing is another
technique that was used here.

01:26:04.310 --> 01:26:06.350
So the idea behind
label smoothing

01:26:06.350 --> 01:26:11.630
is when you tell your model
to predict the next words.

01:26:11.630 --> 01:26:17.490
Instead of saying-- let's
say what a nice day.

01:26:17.490 --> 01:26:20.230
Instead of saying, OK, I want
you to predict the word "day"

01:26:20.230 --> 01:26:25.110
100%, your model is saying you
can predict the word "day--"

01:26:25.110 --> 01:26:26.410
actually, not 100%.

01:26:26.410 --> 01:26:30.190
You can have also other words,
because in language, you

01:26:30.190 --> 01:26:33.750
can complete things in different
ways and still be valid.

01:26:33.750 --> 01:26:37.570
So you can say, what a nice
day, what a nice class,

01:26:37.570 --> 01:26:41.110
what a nice evening.

01:26:41.110 --> 01:26:46.190
Anyways, so label
smoothing takes

01:26:46.190 --> 01:26:52.270
these labels, and it
softens it by saying,

01:26:52.270 --> 01:26:55.190
OK, so instead of
your model needing

01:26:55.190 --> 01:27:01.950
to predict that label, as in you
need to predict that model 100%,

01:27:01.950 --> 01:27:08.360
you say, OK, actually, the
label that I wanted to predict

01:27:08.360 --> 01:27:10.480
is maybe, I don't know, 90%.

01:27:10.480 --> 01:27:13.700
And then the remaining 10%
is all the other words,

01:27:13.700 --> 01:27:16.360
in the vocabulary.

01:27:16.360 --> 01:27:20.380
So it just allows your model
to generalize a bit better.

01:27:20.380 --> 01:27:23.520
And authors have
found that this also

01:27:23.520 --> 01:27:28.000
improves metrics when it
comes to machine translation.

01:27:28.000 --> 01:27:31.660
So for those of you who know
BLEU is one such metric.

01:27:34.200 --> 01:27:38.480
And with that, I'll
give it to Shervine.

01:27:38.480 --> 01:27:40.120
Thank you, Afshine.

01:27:40.120 --> 01:27:42.480
So in this last
part, our goal is

01:27:42.480 --> 01:27:46.280
going to piece all that
Afshine talked about together

01:27:46.280 --> 01:27:50.200
in one single example and see
exactly how this computation

01:27:50.200 --> 01:27:53.920
works in a precise example.

01:27:53.920 --> 01:27:57.400
So as Afshine mentioned,
the original transformer

01:27:57.400 --> 01:28:00.220
is in the business of
machine translation.

01:28:00.220 --> 01:28:04.140
So what we're going to take
is a sentence in English,

01:28:04.140 --> 01:28:06.340
and we're going to
try to translate it

01:28:06.340 --> 01:28:07.860
into some other language.

01:28:07.860 --> 01:28:10.540
So in the example of the
original transformer paper,

01:28:10.540 --> 01:28:12.080
they took French and German.

01:28:12.080 --> 01:28:14.060
So let's take that.

01:28:14.060 --> 01:28:17.000
And let's start with our
favorite toy example,

01:28:17.000 --> 01:28:19.140
so "A cute Teddy
bear is reading."

01:28:19.140 --> 01:28:23.020
And as you recall, the
first step in all of this

01:28:23.020 --> 01:28:24.820
is tokenization.

01:28:24.820 --> 01:28:27.940
And Afshine mentioned
that it's a way

01:28:27.940 --> 01:28:33.260
to arbitrarily split these
words into atomic entities.

01:28:33.260 --> 01:28:37.620
So Afshine mentioned subword
is an optimal way to do it.

01:28:37.620 --> 01:28:39.200
But for the sake
of this example,

01:28:39.200 --> 01:28:41.340
I'm going to take
another split just

01:28:41.340 --> 01:28:43.220
to prove that it's arbitrary.

01:28:43.220 --> 01:28:46.220
So let's say we
split it like this.

01:28:46.220 --> 01:28:49.620
As Afshine mentioned, we
have also special tokens.

01:28:49.620 --> 01:28:55.100
So beginning of sentence and
end of sequence that you put

01:28:55.100 --> 01:28:59.960
to characterize where your
sequence starts and ends.

01:28:59.960 --> 01:29:03.560
Now let's look at
how we encode them.

01:29:03.560 --> 01:29:08.960
So we have, in
this architecture,

01:29:08.960 --> 01:29:12.800
a lookup table that is
made of learnable weights.

01:29:12.800 --> 01:29:14.900
So this is what we
call embeddings.

01:29:14.900 --> 01:29:19.160
And these will be learned as
part of the learning process.

01:29:19.160 --> 01:29:22.680
And as someone
mentioned it here,

01:29:22.680 --> 01:29:26.200
if you want to perform attention
in a way that's context aware

01:29:26.200 --> 01:29:28.920
and that you know where
other tokens are positioned,

01:29:28.920 --> 01:29:31.680
you need that
position information.

01:29:31.680 --> 01:29:34.560
And the original
transformer paper

01:29:34.560 --> 01:29:39.820
puts this position information
at the beginning of the input.

01:29:39.820 --> 01:29:42.520
So you add it additively.

01:29:42.520 --> 01:29:47.520
So let's say you call your
embedding of size D model.

01:29:47.520 --> 01:29:50.080
Then your position embedding
that Afshine described

01:29:50.080 --> 01:29:54.040
by a series of sines and
cosines of different frequencies

01:29:54.040 --> 01:29:56.820
will be a vector
of the same length

01:29:56.820 --> 01:30:00.460
and its additive operation.

01:30:00.460 --> 01:30:04.020
So at this stage, you
end up with an embedding

01:30:04.020 --> 01:30:06.720
that's at least half learnable.

01:30:06.720 --> 01:30:08.940
So the lookup table
that I mentioned to you

01:30:08.940 --> 01:30:10.280
is at least learnable.

01:30:10.280 --> 01:30:14.300
Maybe the positions as well
depends on your design choice.

01:30:14.300 --> 01:30:19.540
And this is what you will put
as an input to your encoder.

01:30:19.540 --> 01:30:21.260
So what happens next?

01:30:21.260 --> 01:30:24.380
Every token has
this representation,

01:30:24.380 --> 01:30:28.180
and you can represent it
in a nice matrix form.

01:30:28.180 --> 01:30:30.180
Let's go with it.

01:30:30.180 --> 01:30:33.060
And you're going to
go into the encoder.

01:30:33.060 --> 01:30:36.540
And as we have seen, the
first component of the encoder

01:30:36.540 --> 01:30:40.900
is going to be that
self-attention layer.

01:30:40.900 --> 01:30:47.600
So you project these inputs
into the space of queries.

01:30:47.600 --> 01:30:52.300
So for that, you have
this Wq projection matrix.

01:30:52.300 --> 01:30:54.020
You get queries.

01:30:54.020 --> 01:30:56.510
With the same
initial embeddings,

01:30:56.510 --> 01:30:58.610
you projected on keys.

01:30:58.610 --> 01:31:02.790
So you have a Wk
projection matrix

01:31:02.790 --> 01:31:06.230
that you will learn to
project it into keys K.

01:31:06.230 --> 01:31:13.390
And you do the same with
values V. OK, great.

01:31:13.390 --> 01:31:17.390
And then based on that,
you apply the formula

01:31:17.390 --> 01:31:21.390
that we mentioned that is so
important, that softmax formula.

01:31:21.390 --> 01:31:24.810
And we do this matrix products.

01:31:24.810 --> 01:31:28.230
And then we obtain
another matrix

01:31:28.230 --> 01:31:34.070
that is of same dimension
as the matrix V.

01:31:34.070 --> 01:31:38.090
So before we dive deeper
into more specifics,

01:31:38.090 --> 01:31:42.430
I just want for us to
get on the same page

01:31:42.430 --> 01:31:45.930
on dimensions, because we talked
about a lot of embeddings here.

01:31:45.930 --> 01:31:47.330
We project things.

01:31:47.330 --> 01:31:48.830
And I just want to
make sure that we

01:31:48.830 --> 01:31:55.610
speak the same language as to
what size are these objects.

01:31:55.610 --> 01:32:01.890
So your input sequence-- so
we mentioned it's of length n.

01:32:01.890 --> 01:32:06.250
And one notation that people use
to characterize these embedding

01:32:06.250 --> 01:32:09.290
size is D model.

01:32:09.290 --> 01:32:13.810
And then what the projection
matrices of Q, K and V

01:32:13.810 --> 01:32:18.690
do is respectively, you
start from the D model world,

01:32:18.690 --> 01:32:22.570
and you turn it into
the query world,

01:32:22.570 --> 01:32:25.310
and then key worlds,
and then value world.

01:32:25.310 --> 01:32:29.890
So this is why it's of
size D model and DQ.

01:32:29.890 --> 01:32:34.530
And similarly, D
model Dk, D model DV

01:32:34.530 --> 01:32:37.170
for each projection matrix.

01:32:37.170 --> 01:32:41.490
And you obtain these
queries, keys, and values

01:32:41.490 --> 01:32:44.970
that are of size n rows
but that are this time,

01:32:44.970 --> 01:32:48.610
into these query,
key, and value space.

01:32:48.610 --> 01:32:54.390
So the number of columns
is DQ, DK, and DV.

01:32:54.390 --> 01:32:57.630
And since you do,
this matrix multiply,

01:32:57.630 --> 01:33:02.270
you need these constraints
that DQ is equal to DK.

01:33:02.270 --> 01:33:04.750
So here, I put DQ and
DK just for the sake

01:33:04.750 --> 01:33:06.650
of properly naming things.

01:33:06.650 --> 01:33:12.630
But DQ should be equal
to DK by construction.

01:33:12.630 --> 01:33:15.910
And this is what you get.

01:33:15.910 --> 01:33:18.430
All good so far?

01:33:18.430 --> 01:33:19.710
OK, great.

01:33:19.710 --> 01:33:25.750
So now, let's pause a second
and then go back to this example

01:33:25.750 --> 01:33:29.830
and see how the matrix-matrix
computation work.

01:33:29.830 --> 01:33:33.110
So as Afshine was
mentioning earlier,

01:33:33.110 --> 01:33:39.190
each token can be represented
as a row embedding.

01:33:39.190 --> 01:33:44.710
And you have that for Q. If
you consider K transpose,

01:33:44.710 --> 01:33:49.160
each column is going to be the
embedding of a given vector.

01:33:49.160 --> 01:33:51.520
So when you do the
matrix multiply,

01:33:51.520 --> 01:33:56.120
you're going to end up
with a matrix of size n

01:33:56.120 --> 01:34:02.620
by n, where each cell is
going to be some similarity.

01:34:02.620 --> 01:34:06.520
Like here, it's a dot
product between a given

01:34:06.520 --> 01:34:11.400
query and a given key.

01:34:11.400 --> 01:34:14.360
And let's suppose
here, you multiply this

01:34:14.360 --> 01:34:19.320
by V. What you're going to
end up with is for each row,

01:34:19.320 --> 01:34:21.080
you have a given query.

01:34:21.080 --> 01:34:24.600
You project it on
the space of keys

01:34:24.600 --> 01:34:25.940
that you have in your sentence.

01:34:25.940 --> 01:34:29.400
So you get some-- so you
want to get some probability

01:34:29.400 --> 01:34:32.000
distribution over keys.

01:34:32.000 --> 01:34:38.560
And then you're going to
use these weights to weight

01:34:38.560 --> 01:34:39.520
the values.

01:34:39.520 --> 01:34:41.800
And then you're going to
do a sum of all of this.

01:34:41.800 --> 01:34:44.160
Now you're going to say,
OK, Shervine and Afshine,

01:34:44.160 --> 01:34:48.700
you talk about QK transpose
V, QK transpose V. This is not

01:34:48.700 --> 01:34:49.640
what the formula is.

01:34:49.640 --> 01:34:51.340
And then you are right.

01:34:51.340 --> 01:34:53.480
The formula is
approximately this.

01:34:53.480 --> 01:34:55.700
We take the softmax
in order to get

01:34:55.700 --> 01:34:58.820
a probability distribution
that sums to 1

01:34:58.820 --> 01:35:00.820
and has nice properties.

01:35:00.820 --> 01:35:02.840
And one thing we
haven't mentioned,

01:35:02.840 --> 01:35:05.780
but that the authors
chose, is that you

01:35:05.780 --> 01:35:12.460
will normalize by some factor
because the dimension decay will

01:35:12.460 --> 01:35:15.520
influence the variance that you
might get as part of the sum.

01:35:15.520 --> 01:35:19.020
So if you want everything
to be properly normalized,

01:35:19.020 --> 01:35:22.620
you can divide by the
standard deviation.

01:35:22.620 --> 01:35:26.460
And this is what happens here.

01:35:26.460 --> 01:35:31.680
And so, as we mentioned, this
is a multi-headed process.

01:35:31.680 --> 01:35:35.900
So you do not do this process
once you do it h times.

01:35:35.900 --> 01:35:40.340
So this process of learning
queries, keys, and values

01:35:40.340 --> 01:35:41.780
is done in parallel.

01:35:41.780 --> 01:35:44.520
And then at the
end, you concatenate

01:35:44.520 --> 01:35:51.760
all these softmax of QK
transpose over square root of DK

01:35:51.760 --> 01:35:54.000
times V, together.

01:35:54.000 --> 01:35:56.560
And then you have a
last projection matrix

01:35:56.560 --> 01:36:01.840
that we call W0 that will turn
these embeddings in a space

01:36:01.840 --> 01:36:07.240
HDV back into D model.

01:36:07.240 --> 01:36:09.120
So you have this nice
property, by the way,

01:36:09.120 --> 01:36:12.560
in the transformer, where
a lot of the operations

01:36:12.560 --> 01:36:16.240
make the original dimension
of embeddings invariant.

01:36:16.240 --> 01:36:18.800
So you start with D
model, you do a bunch

01:36:18.800 --> 01:36:21.540
of attention calculations.

01:36:21.540 --> 01:36:23.480
You end up with D model.

01:36:23.480 --> 01:36:25.500
And then you go
into the next layer,

01:36:25.500 --> 01:36:28.000
which is a feedforward
neural network.

01:36:28.000 --> 01:36:31.160
That is a one
hidden layer network

01:36:31.160 --> 01:36:36.240
that projects this D model
representation into a DFF1.

01:36:36.240 --> 01:36:38.600
And then typically, you would
have this dimension that

01:36:38.600 --> 01:36:41.200
is bigger than
the model in order

01:36:41.200 --> 01:36:46.330
to allow for learning
complex representations.

01:36:46.330 --> 01:36:50.430
And you do all of
that big N time.

01:36:50.430 --> 01:36:54.230
So in the original transformer
paper, big N is equal to 6.

01:36:54.230 --> 01:36:55.810
So you do that a bunch of times.

01:36:55.810 --> 01:36:59.250
And then at the
end of the encoder,

01:36:59.250 --> 01:37:02.610
you have for each
token, a representation

01:37:02.610 --> 01:37:10.290
that represents that token in a
context aware and learned way.

01:37:10.290 --> 01:37:11.790
Is everything good so far?

01:37:14.730 --> 01:37:15.612
OK, awesome.

01:37:15.612 --> 01:37:18.070
So I'm asking because we're
going to switch to the decoder.

01:37:18.070 --> 01:37:21.890
So I just want to make
sure all make sense.

01:37:21.890 --> 01:37:27.410
So now that we have
that, as we mentioned,

01:37:27.410 --> 01:37:29.650
the original transformer
is in the business

01:37:29.650 --> 01:37:30.990
of machine translation.

01:37:30.990 --> 01:37:33.130
So you need to
start the beginning

01:37:33.130 --> 01:37:35.250
of your translated sentence.

01:37:35.250 --> 01:37:38.250
And one way to force
the start of it

01:37:38.250 --> 01:37:41.810
is to have this is
placeholder token.

01:37:41.810 --> 01:37:43.910
So you add this BOS token.

01:37:43.910 --> 01:37:46.170
Look at the lookup table
of your embeddings.

01:37:46.170 --> 01:37:47.990
You have an initial embedding.

01:37:47.990 --> 01:37:51.130
You add your position
embedding to it,

01:37:51.130 --> 01:37:53.550
and then you pass
it to the decoder.

01:37:53.550 --> 01:37:57.150
And you have this
self-attention layer

01:37:57.150 --> 01:38:00.290
that Afshine mentioned is a
masked self-attention layer,

01:38:00.290 --> 01:38:03.710
because what happens
here is that it's causal.

01:38:03.710 --> 01:38:10.150
Every token looks at itself
and the tokens that precede it.

01:38:10.150 --> 01:38:15.350
And once that happens, you
have another attention layer

01:38:15.350 --> 01:38:18.510
that's called
cross-attention, where

01:38:18.510 --> 01:38:21.870
the query is the decoder token.

01:38:21.870 --> 01:38:26.310
And then the keys and
the values are the tokens

01:38:26.310 --> 01:38:28.550
that come from the encoder.

01:38:28.550 --> 01:38:30.290
So you look at the
stack of encoders.

01:38:30.290 --> 01:38:32.910
You look at the
output embedding.

01:38:32.910 --> 01:38:37.870
And in every layer of
decoder, every decoder block

01:38:37.870 --> 01:38:42.850
will get that last embedding
as keys and values.

01:38:42.850 --> 01:38:44.610
So you do that,
and then you have,

01:38:44.610 --> 01:38:47.010
just like in the
encoder, a layer

01:38:47.010 --> 01:38:50.650
of feedforward neural network
that allows for complex feature

01:38:50.650 --> 01:38:52.290
learning.

01:38:52.290 --> 01:38:54.030
And all of that goes.

01:38:54.030 --> 01:38:57.170
And then you have some
linear layer and then

01:38:57.170 --> 01:39:01.490
a softmax layer that will
turn these representations

01:39:01.490 --> 01:39:06.010
into a probability distribution
over the space of your words--

01:39:06.010 --> 01:39:09.090
or your tokens over
the vocabulary space

01:39:09.090 --> 01:39:11.690
to predict the next word.

01:39:11.690 --> 01:39:13.530
And then what happens
here is that-- we

01:39:13.530 --> 01:39:15.890
will see in the
next lectures what

01:39:15.890 --> 01:39:18.810
could be heuristics
to pick the next word.

01:39:18.810 --> 01:39:21.490
But let's say here,
we pick the max.

01:39:21.490 --> 01:39:22.470
So you pick the max.

01:39:22.470 --> 01:39:24.570
You know what word
we have predicted.

01:39:24.570 --> 01:39:28.410
And then you put it back
as an input to the decoder.

01:39:28.410 --> 01:39:31.890
And you do the exact
same process again.

01:39:31.890 --> 01:39:35.270
So here, we put
beginning of sequence.

01:39:35.270 --> 01:39:36.510
You get "un."

01:39:36.510 --> 01:39:41.150
And then you get the next token
[FRENCH] which is the equivalent

01:39:41.150 --> 01:39:44.870
of "Teddy bear," and so on.

01:39:44.870 --> 01:39:50.150
And then you get, at
the end, your sequence

01:39:50.150 --> 01:39:51.590
in the target language.

01:39:51.590 --> 01:39:56.572
And then you do that until you
hit an end of sequence token.

01:39:56.572 --> 01:39:59.030
This is where you know that
you should stop the translation

01:39:59.030 --> 01:40:01.110
process.

01:40:01.110 --> 01:40:06.650
So this is how we start from a
sentence in a given language,

01:40:06.650 --> 01:40:10.430
and we end up in one
in the target language.

01:40:10.430 --> 01:40:14.490
So any questions on this so far?

01:40:18.630 --> 01:40:20.190
Yep.

01:40:20.190 --> 01:40:23.410
The comment here is it
all looks complicated.

01:40:23.410 --> 01:40:26.170
If you were to boil down
exactly what makes it work,

01:40:26.170 --> 01:40:27.350
what would it be?

01:40:27.350 --> 01:40:30.710
So "Attention is All You
Need," the title of the paper,

01:40:30.710 --> 01:40:34.150
translates a bit
the secret sauce.

01:40:34.150 --> 01:40:37.720
So the attention
layer here is key.

01:40:37.720 --> 01:40:40.560
We're going to see in the
next lectures that actually,

01:40:40.560 --> 01:40:42.840
a lot of these
things are dropped.

01:40:42.840 --> 01:40:45.720
And we rely on a subset
of these components

01:40:45.720 --> 01:40:48.800
that we repeat again and again.

01:40:48.800 --> 01:40:51.340
So this attention
layer is the key.

01:40:51.340 --> 01:40:54.240
And then the second thing is
this feedforward neural network

01:40:54.240 --> 01:40:57.380
where most of the parameters
of the network reside.

01:40:57.380 --> 01:40:59.760
So if you were to calculate
exactly the parameters

01:40:59.760 --> 01:41:01.960
of your model, you
realize that these

01:41:01.960 --> 01:41:04.180
contain most of the capacity.

01:41:04.180 --> 01:41:06.360
And this is where
the learning happens.

01:41:06.360 --> 01:41:09.560
So if you were to ask me,
these two, and then the rest

01:41:09.560 --> 01:41:14.700
are just tricks to better
learn to improve all of it,

01:41:14.700 --> 01:41:17.040
and then, of course, the
quality of the data, which

01:41:17.040 --> 01:41:20.240
is another layer of complexity.

01:41:20.240 --> 01:41:22.720
Yeah.

01:41:22.720 --> 01:41:27.360
So the question is, can you
talk more about the masked part?

01:41:27.360 --> 01:41:31.120
So it's a computational
trick where

01:41:31.120 --> 01:41:33.260
you have a triangular
matrix that

01:41:33.260 --> 01:41:37.940
will mask some connections
of your self-attention layer.

01:41:37.940 --> 01:41:41.180
And at training time,
it prevents connections

01:41:41.180 --> 01:41:45.940
that you shouldn't
know to be made.

01:41:45.940 --> 01:41:49.697
So yeah, that's the gist of it.

01:41:49.697 --> 01:41:50.280
Yeah, exactly.

01:41:50.280 --> 01:41:52.760
So yeah, the question is
how it relates to softmax?

01:41:52.760 --> 01:41:54.120
So you have minus infinities.

01:41:54.120 --> 01:41:56.020
And then this will be 0.

01:41:56.020 --> 01:41:58.900
Softmax of minus
infinity will be 0.

01:41:58.900 --> 01:42:01.640
This is how it relates.

01:42:01.640 --> 01:42:02.140
Yeah.

01:42:02.140 --> 01:42:05.420
But these are more
computational tricks.

01:42:05.420 --> 01:42:08.180
I think the main
thing to have in mind

01:42:08.180 --> 01:42:11.780
is to forget about all
connections from a given

01:42:11.780 --> 01:42:14.900
token and all the
ones that succeeded.

01:42:14.900 --> 01:42:19.340
I think that's good
enough as a mental model.

01:42:19.340 --> 01:42:20.860
Yeah.

01:42:20.860 --> 01:42:21.660
OK, awesome.

01:42:21.660 --> 01:42:23.060
Yeah.

01:42:23.060 --> 01:42:26.320
The question here is on
various uses of softmax.

01:42:26.320 --> 01:42:31.160
So the ones that are used
in the self-attention layers

01:42:31.160 --> 01:42:34.720
and the cross-attention layers
have a different function

01:42:34.720 --> 01:42:36.920
than the one at the last layer.

01:42:36.920 --> 01:42:39.160
You should see the ones
in the attention layer

01:42:39.160 --> 01:42:42.640
as a projection of the query
over the space of keys.

01:42:42.640 --> 01:42:47.240
So what we do is projecting the
representation of a given token,

01:42:47.240 --> 01:42:50.760
so cross tokens,
whereas the last softmax

01:42:50.760 --> 01:42:52.620
layer has a different function.

01:42:52.620 --> 01:42:57.880
It's one that tries for you
to predict the next token.

01:42:57.880 --> 01:43:02.080
So in some sense,
yeah, so I do get the--

01:43:02.080 --> 01:43:03.680
I think it's a great
point that we're

01:43:03.680 --> 01:43:05.490
using several times,
this function,

01:43:05.490 --> 01:43:06.740
but it's for different things.

01:43:09.760 --> 01:43:11.720
I think there was-- yeah.

01:43:11.720 --> 01:43:14.132
So the question is, why
is the translation not

01:43:14.132 --> 01:43:15.340
having beginning of sequence?

01:43:15.340 --> 01:43:16.520
It does.

01:43:16.520 --> 01:43:18.720
Yeah, it's the first token here.

01:43:18.720 --> 01:43:19.220
Oh, yeah.

01:43:19.220 --> 01:43:22.880
So the first token here
is a placeholder for you

01:43:22.880 --> 01:43:25.580
to predict the next token
that comes after it.

01:43:25.580 --> 01:43:28.200
So it doesn't come at the
output of the decoder.

01:43:28.200 --> 01:43:30.050
It's just the input
of the decoder.

01:43:30.050 --> 01:43:32.370
And then what you get as
the output of the decoder

01:43:32.370 --> 01:43:35.530
that you should watch
out is end of sequence

01:43:35.530 --> 01:43:38.250
because this is where
you should stop.

01:43:38.250 --> 01:43:40.050
Yeah.

01:43:40.050 --> 01:43:41.470
OK, awesome.

01:43:41.470 --> 01:43:44.370
And then with all that,
I want to tell you

01:43:44.370 --> 01:43:48.790
that what we presented
today is just the beginning.

01:43:48.790 --> 01:43:51.850
We're just in 2017
at this point.

01:43:51.850 --> 01:43:54.010
And there has been,
as I'm sure you know,

01:43:54.010 --> 01:43:58.210
many uses of this
technology that now powers

01:43:58.210 --> 01:44:01.290
all these chatbots that we use.

01:44:01.290 --> 01:44:04.810
So we're going to cover
how we train such models

01:44:04.810 --> 01:44:06.330
in the next few lectures.

01:44:06.330 --> 01:44:08.310
And then in the second
part of the class,

01:44:08.310 --> 01:44:11.370
we will tackle how
these can power

01:44:11.370 --> 01:44:14.530
the agents that we
interact and use today

01:44:14.530 --> 01:44:17.810
and how it can be used
in several systems.

01:44:17.810 --> 01:44:20.410
And with that, thank
you for your attention.

01:44:20.410 --> 01:44:22.840
And I hope you have
a great weekend.

